"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_tma_site=self.webpackChunk_tma_site||[]).push([[5313],{11424(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/2026/11/14/phase-11","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/14/phase-11","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-14-phase-11.md","title":"Phase 11: retraining T2 on realistic bearing error does not improve its range","description":"Phases 7\u201310 showed that under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting bias), the","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.3,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 11: retraining T2 on realistic bearing error does not improve its range","date":"2026-11-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 10: T2\'s range with a filter\'s velocity passes G8, but adds little that was not known","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/12/phase-10"}},"content":"Phases 7\u201310 showed that under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting bias), the\\nlearned network T2 gives the most robust range estimate. T2 had been trained only on white noise.\\nThe obvious next question: does training it on the realistic error make it better still?\\n\\n## The test\\n\\n- **The model.** T2\'s architecture and configuration, unchanged, trained from scratch on a fresh\\n  100,000-scenario dataset generated entirely with realistic error. Training took about 16 hours\\n  on GitHub Actions and stopped early at epoch 48.\\n- **The gate (G8).** The retrained T2 must:\\n  - have a median range error wholly below the frozen T2\'s;\\n  - pass G5 against the reference IMM.\\n- **The validation step first.** The six point estimates on the shared validation split must all be\\n  within their limits before any test data are generated.\\n\\n## Result: no gain on range, so it stops at validation\\n\\n| Retrained T2 against | Range                    | Course | Settled course |\\n| -------------------- | ------------------------ | ------ | -------------- |\\n| The frozen T2        | **1.021** [0.991, 1.052] | 0.981  | **0.878**      |\\n| The IMM              | 0.745                    | 0.857  | 1.079          |\\n\\n- **Range is unchanged.** 12.4% against the frozen T2\'s 12.2%, and the interval also rules out a\\n  gain of more than 1%.\\n- **Settled course improves** by about 12%, mostly on straight legs before any manoeuvre.\\n- **It still beats the IMM** on overall range and course, and passes G5\'s settled limits.\\n\\n## The cost: worse on white noise\\n\\nOn the same scenarios with white noise only, the retrained T2 is clearly worse:\\n\\n| Median range error | White noise | Realistic error |\\n| ------------------ | ----------- | --------------- |\\n| Retrained T2       | 10.3%       | 12.4%           |\\n| Frozen T2          | **8.0%**    | 12.2%           |\\n| IMM                | 9.1%        | 16.7%           |\\n\\nThe retrained T2 looks more robust (it degrades 1.21 \xd7 against 1.52 \xd7), but only because it starts\\nworse. On white noise it loses to the IMM on range.\\n\\n## Baselines (realistic error, all gated rows)\\n\\n- **Batch least squares:** 17.1% and 46.0\xb0. It is best before any manoeuvre (6.9%, 2.0\xb0).\\n- **EKF:** 16.1% and 29.7\xb0. It is best on settled course (9.8\xb0) and speed (1.91 kt).\\n- **Naive:** 38.7% and 88.3\xb0.\\n- **Fused tracker and hybrid:** rebuilt on the retrained T2, they are unchanged (within 0.3\\n  points in range).\\n\\n## What it means\\n\\nSeeing the error in training did not buy range accuracy. A plausible reading is that the frozen\\nT2 is already near what the bearings allow under this error model: phase 9 found the drifting bias\\nnearly unobservable, and a network cannot learn what the data do not contain.\\n\\n**Limits:**\\n\\n- one training run, so the run-to-run spread is unknown;\\n- one error model at one severity;\\n- validation data only;\\n- an architecture that was not re-tuned.\\n\\nNo test data were generated. Phase 10\'s verdict stands, and the owner decides what comes next.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261114-p11-val/` (diagnostic, baselines, clean twin); model\\n`experiments/20261113-p11a-models/`; decision `experiments/20261113-p11-decision/`; spec 023; SRD\\nAnnex C \xa7C.18."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/phase-10","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/12/phase-10","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-phase-10.md","title":"Phase 10: T2\'s range with a filter\'s velocity passes G8, but adds little that was not known","description":"Phases 7\u20139 left one clear lesson. Under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.25,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 10: T2\'s range with a filter\'s velocity passes G8, but adds little that was not known","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 11: retraining T2 on realistic bearing error does not improve its range","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/14/phase-11"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 9: a bias-aware IMM cannot see the bias","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/12/phase-9"}},"content":"Phases 7\u20139 left one clear lesson. Under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting\\nbias), the learned network T2 gives the most robust range, and no change to the filters closes\\nthe gap. Phase 10 takes the direct route and reports T2\'s range alongside the fused tracker\'s\\ncourse and speed.\\n\\n## The test\\n\\n- **The estimator:**\\n  - range and position from T2;\\n  - course and speed from the frozen fused tracker;\\n  - T2\'s whole row wherever the fused tracker has no estimate (0.9% of the gated rows, 2.5% of\\n    all rows).\\n\\n  Nothing is trained or tuned.\\n\\n- **The gate (G8):** pass G5 against the reference IMM, **and** have a median range error wholly\\n  below the fused tracker\'s.\\n- **Pre-registration.** The rules, the validation run and the decision record were all committed\\n  before the test data existed.\\n\\n## Result: G8 passes (seed 224, 40,000 scenarios)\\n\\n| Hybrid against              | Range                    | Course    | Settled range | Settled course | Settled speed |\\n| --------------------------- | ------------------------ | --------- | ------------- | -------------- | ------------- |\\n| The IMM (limit 1 / 1.1)     | **0.689**                | **0.895** | 0.724         | 0.887          | 0.898         |\\n| The fused tracker (limit 1) | **0.908** [0.893, 0.921] |           |               |                |               |\\n\\nThe checks use 67,292 rows from the 20,161 test scenarios at \u03c3 = 1\xb0. T2 converged on every row.\\nEvery 97.5% interval sits wholly within its limit. Five independent reviewers agreed that the\\nverdict is right.\\n\\n## Why this is mostly a replay\\n\\nThe panel was blunt about how much this pass adds:\\n\\n- **The range is T2\'s, row for row.** The check against the fused tracker is therefore T2 against\\n  the fused tracker. Phase 7 already showed that (fused/T2 1.095), and phase 8 showed no tuning\\n  closes it. Seed 224 replicates that result.\\n- **The velocity is the fused tracker\'s,** which already passed G5 in phase 7.\\n- **The estimator to beat changed.** In phases 8\u20139 it was T2; here it is the fused tracker, by the\\n  owner\'s choice before validation. T2 is still not beaten on range.\\n- **What is new** is only that the pair, assembled per output, passes every G5 check at once.\\n- **About 1 point of each margin comes from the scoring convention.** A row with no estimate counts\\n  as infinitely wrong, and the hybrid never lacks one. On rows where every estimator converged the\\n  ratios are 0.700 against the IMM and 0.918 against the fused tracker. Both still pass.\\n\\n## What it does not show\\n\\n| Median error, all G5 rows | Range | Course    | Speed       |\\n| ------------------------- | ----- | --------- | ----------- |\\n| Hybrid                    | 12.0% | 26.6\xb0     | 2.58 kt     |\\n| Fused tracker             | 13.2% | 27.1\xb0     | 2.60 kt     |\\n| T2 alone                  | 12.0% | **25.9\xb0** | 2.85 kt     |\\n| IMM                       | 17.4% | 29.7\xb0     | 2.87 kt     |\\n| EKF                       | 16.6% | 30.2\xb0     | **2.41 kt** |\\n| Batch least squares       | 18.0% | 46.6\xb0     | 2.50 kt     |\\n| Naive                     | 40.2% | 89.9\xb0     | 3.99 kt     |\\n\\n- **No gain over T2 on range,** since the range is T2\'s. T2 alone is also slightly better on overall\\n  course.\\n- **Classical filters still win on straight legs.** The EKF is better on settled course (10.7\xb0\\n  against 13.3\xb0) and speed. Before any manoeuvre, batch least squares is best (7.3%, 2.2\xb0).\\n- **It is not a track.** The position comes from one estimator and the velocity from another. The\\n  position change over a minute disagrees with the stated velocity by a median 18 kt (8 kt for the\\n  fused tracker; 17 kt for T2 alone, whose position track is itself jumpy), and the implied bearing rate is the worst of the four estimators. It is a set of\\n  per-output estimates, not a state you could extrapolate.\\n- **No uncertainty** is stated for the combined estimate.\\n- **Scope:** one error model at one severity, on simulated data.\\n\\n## The arc, phases 7\u201310\\n\\nUnder realistic bearing error:\\n\\n- the learned range estimate is the most robust;\\n- feeding it into a filter costs some of that robustness;\\n- tuning the fusion doesn\'t recover it;\\n- modelling the bias inside the filter fails, because the bias can\'t be observed.\\n\\nReporting the learned range directly keeps its accuracy, at the cost of a coherent track.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261112-p10-g8/` (verdict, baselines, coherence, panel review); validation\\n`experiments/20261112-p10-val/`; decision `experiments/20261112-p10-decision/`; spec 022."},{"id":"/2026/11/12/phase-9","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/12/phase-9","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-12-phase-9.md","title":"Phase 9: a bias-aware IMM cannot see the bias","description":"Under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting bias), the learned range estimate","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":1.76,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 9: a bias-aware IMM cannot see the bias","date":"2026-11-12T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 10: T2\'s range with a filter\'s velocity passes G8, but adds little that was not known","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/12/phase-10"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 8: re-tuning the fusion cannot make it beat T2 on range under realistic error","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/10/phase-8"}},"content":"Under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting bias), the learned range estimate\\nT2 beat the classical IMM, and no fusion setting could close the gap (phase 8). The natural\\nclassical answer is to model the error: give the IMM a sixth state for the bearing bias and let\\nit estimate the bias.\\n\\n## The test\\n\\n- **The filter:** the reference IMM, plus a drifting bias added to every predicted bearing.\\n- **Tuning:** the bias\'s size and time constant and the measurement noise scale, 18 settings in\\n  all, on validation data with realistic error.\\n- **The rule, fixed before any setting was scored:** to go to a gate, a setting must beat T2 on\\n  range and still pass G5 against the plain IMM.\\n- **The forecast**, recorded beforehand: this would probably fail. A quick check had shown that a\\n  1\xb0 offset barely changes how well a straight-line track fits the bearings.\\n\\n## Result: it stops at validation\\n\\n| Bias-state IMM                    | vs plain IMM, range | vs T2, range |\\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------- | ------------ |\\n| Best setting (0.25\xb0, 10 min, r 8) | 0.998               | 1.37         |\\n| Larger bias (1\xb0)                  | 1.05\u20131.12           | 1.44\u20131.53    |\\n\\n- **At best it matches the plain IMM.** It never improves on it.\\n- **It stays about 1.4 \xd7 T2\'s range error.**\\n- **Allowing a larger bias makes it worse.** The extra freedom is not pinned down by the bearings.\\n\\n## Why\\n\\nFrom bearings alone, a slowly drifting bias looks almost exactly like a slightly rotated target\\ntrack. One or two ownship zigs do not separate the two, so the filter cannot learn the bias. T2\\ndoes better on range, plausibly because it learns range from the whole bearing history. That\\nexplanation was not tested.\\n\\n## Where this leaves things\\n\\nPhases 7\u20139 tell one story. Under this error model:\\n\\n- the learned range is the most robust estimate;\\n- fusing it into a filter that assumes white noise loses some of that advantage;\\n- modelling the bias inside the filter does not help, because the bias cannot be observed.\\n\\nTwo options remain for the owner:\\n\\n- report T2\'s range directly, as a hybrid;\\n- close out the project.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261112-p9-research/`; spec 021; SRD Annex C \xa7C.16."},{"id":"/2026/11/10/phase-8","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/10/phase-8","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-10-phase-8.md","title":"Phase 8: re-tuning the fusion cannot make it beat T2 on range under realistic error","description":"Phase 7 found a gap. Under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting bias), T2 on","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.09,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 8: re-tuning the fusion cannot make it beat T2 on range under realistic error","date":"2026-11-10T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 9: a bias-aware IMM cannot see the bias","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/12/phase-9"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 7: realistic bearing error \u2014 the frozen fused tracker still beats the frozen IMM, but T2 alone is more robust","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/08/phase-7"}},"content":"Phase 7 found a gap. Under realistic bearing error (correlated noise plus a drifting bias), T2 on\\nits own had lower range error than the fused tracker that feeds T2\'s range into the IMM: 12.1%\\nagainst 13.2%. The fusion\'s weighting had been tuned on clean data and then frozen, so the obvious\\nquestion was whether re-tuning it closes the gap.\\n\\n## The test\\n\\nThe owner chose to re-tune only the fusion\'s two settings, on validation data with realistic error:\\n\\n- k, how much to trust T2\'s range;\\n- M, how often to feed it in.\\n\\nThe gate would require both of these:\\n\\n- **beat T2 alone** on median range error;\\n- **still pass G5** against the reference IMM.\\n\\nThe grid had 24 settings. The rule for choosing between them was written and committed before any\\nof them was scored. A setting went to the gate only if its estimated margin on every check was\\npositive.\\n\\n## Result: no setting qualifies\\n\\n| Setting                                     | Fused / IMM range | Fused / T2 range |\\n| ------------------------------------------- | ----------------- | ---------------- |\\n| Frozen (k 4, every 6 samples)               | 0.777             | 1.064            |\\n| **Best re-tune (k 3, every sample)**        | 0.740             | **1.014**        |\\n| Heaviest weight on T2 (k 0.5, every sample) | 0.749             | 1.027            |\\n\\n- **Re-tuning helps against the IMM.** Feeding T2\'s range at every sample cuts the fused\\n  tracker\'s range error further (0.74 \xd7 the IMM\'s), and cuts its shortfall against T2 from 6.4%\\n  to 1.4%.\\n- **It never beats T2.** The best setting is still 1.4% worse on range. Trusting T2 more does not\\n  help either: course and velocity then fall apart (course up to 1.27 \xd7 the IMM\'s on settled\\n  rows), and range still trails T2.\\n- **So phase 8 stops at validation.** No fresh test data were generated, and phase 7\'s verdict\\n  stands.\\n\\n## Why, plausibly\\n\\nUnder the drifting bias, the IMM\'s range ends up about 7% short. Every bearing update pulls the\\nshared track state back towards that biased range, so a single filter state cannot hold T2\'s less\\nbiased range, however the two are weighted. This explanation was not tested here.\\n\\n## What it means\\n\\nWithin this design, fusing T2\'s range into a filter that assumes white noise costs some range\\naccuracy under correlated error. The filter\'s state stays coherent, and it still beats the IMM\\nclearly.\\n\\nTwo further changes might close the gap:\\n\\n- a filter that models the bias;\\n- reporting T2\'s range directly, as the phase-4b hybrid did.\\n\\nEither would be a new decision for the owner.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261110-p8-research/` (grid, selection, validation note); spec 020; SRD\\nAnnex C \xa7C.15."},{"id":"/2026/11/08/phase-7","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/08/phase-7","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-08-phase-7.md","title":"Phase 7: realistic bearing error \u2014 the frozen fused tracker still beats the frozen IMM, but T2 alone is more robust","description":"So far every tracker in this project has seen only white bearing noise. Real bearings also carry","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.87,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 7: realistic bearing error \u2014 the frozen fused tracker still beats the frozen IMM, but T2 alone is more robust","date":"2026-11-08T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 8: re-tuning the fusion cannot make it beat T2 on range under realistic error","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/10/phase-8"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 6 re-score: G7 passes, with coverage within tolerance on average but not calibrated","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/07/phase-6-rescore"}},"content":"So far every tracker in this project has seen only white bearing noise. Real bearings also carry\\ncorrelated error and a slowly drifting bias. Phase 7 asks one question: **does the fused tracker\'s\\nG5 advantage over the reference IMM survive that error, with nothing re-trained or re-tuned?**\\n\\n## The setup\\n\\nThe owner chose three things:\\n\\n- **frozen trackers;**\\n- **an error model** made of a correlated term (sd 1.0\xb0, 30 s) and a drifting bias (sd 0.5\xb0,\\n  20 min). This setting is double the one first proposed. At \u03c3 = 1\xb0 the total bearing error goes\\n  from 1.0\xb0 to 1.5\xb0;\\n- **G5\'s rules, unchanged.**\\n\\nThe generator adds the error from its own random stream, so each scenario can be regenerated\\nwithout it. The two versions have identical geometry and identical white noise, which makes a\\npaired clean comparison possible.\\n\\n## Result: G5 passes (seed 218, 40,000 scenarios)\\n\\n| Fused / IMM    | Ratio (97.5% CI)         | Limit |\\n| -------------- | ------------------------ | ----- |\\n| Overall range  | **0.766** [0.755, 0.778] | < 1   |\\n| Overall course | **0.907** [0.897, 0.918] | < 1   |\\n| Settled range  | 0.774                    | < 1.1 |\\n| Settled course | 0.875                    | < 1.1 |\\n| Settled speed  | 0.907                    | < 1.1 |\\n\\nValidation on seed 217 had already shown much the same: 0.777 for range and 0.922 for course. Five\\nindependent reviewers agreed the verdict is right, and one reproduced it byte for byte.\\n\\n## What the pass does not show\\n\\nThe absolute errors are large: the fused tracker\'s 90th-percentile course error is 106\xb0. The panel\\nnarrowed the claim.\\n\\n| Median error, all G5 rows | Range     | Course  | Speed      |\\n| ------------------------- | --------- | ------- | ---------- |\\n| Fused tracker             | 13.2%     | 27\xb0     | 2.6 kt     |\\n| IMM (reference)           | 17.3%     | 30\xb0     | 2.9 kt     |\\n| T2 alone                  | **12.1%** | **26\xb0** | 2.9 kt     |\\n| EKF                       | 16.8%     | 31\xb0     | **2.4 kt** |\\n| Batch least squares       | 17.6%     | 47\xb0     | 2.4 kt     |\\n| Naive                     | 40%       | 90\xb0     | 3.9 kt     |\\n\\n- **T2 alone beats the fused tracker on range**, by 1.10 \xd7 [1.08, 1.11] overall. Its error grows\\n  least under realistic error (1.47 \xd7 its clean value, against the IMM\'s 1.82 \xd7). The IMM\'s range\\n  ends up biased about 7% short under the drifting bias, while T2 stays nearly unbiased. The fusion\\n  setting, frozen from clean data, plausibly gives T2 too little weight; k was not re-swept. Fusing keeps one coherent track\\n  state, but under this error it is not the best range estimator.\\n- **On straight legs, classical methods still win.** On settled rows the EKF beats the fused\\n  tracker on course (10.6\xb0 against 13.5\xb0) and on speed, and batch least squares does too (8.1\xb0,\\n  1.8 kt). Before any manoeuvre, batch least squares\\n  is best of all: 7.3% range and 2.2\xb0 course.\\n- **The reference was frozen, not adapted.** Neither filter models correlated or bias error. An\\n  IMM with an inflated measurement noise or a bias state might close the gap; this phase does not\\n  test that.\\n- **Every tracker is much worse.** Median range error rises 1.5\u20131.8 \xd7 overall and about 2 \xd7 on\\n  settled rows.\\n- **Not measured.** Filter consistency (NIS, NEES) and covariance health (non-PD steps) under\\n  correlated error.\\n- **Scope.** The result covers one error model at one severity, \u03c3 = 1\xb0 rows and simulated data.\\n\\n## The one-line version\\n\\nUnder one setting of realistic bearing error, the frozen fused tracker keeps its G5 advantage over\\nthe frozen IMM, which was not adapted to this error. The learned range (T2) is likely what makes it robust (a hypothesis), and on its\\nown T2 is more robust still. The classical filters remain better on straight legs.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261108-p7-g5/` (verdict, baselines, clean comparison, panel review);\\nvalidation `experiments/20261108-p7-val/`; decision `experiments/20261108-p7-decision/`; G0\\n`experiments/20261108-g0-p7-r1/`; spec 019."},{"id":"/2026/11/07/phase-6-rescore","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/07/phase-6-rescore","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-07-phase-6-rescore.md","title":"Phase 6 re-score: G7 passes, with coverage within tolerance on average but not calibrated","description":"G7 on seed 215 was inconclusive: the fused tracker\'s 50% range interval covered 48.3%, and its","date":"2026-11-07T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.61,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 6 re-score: G7 passes, with coverage within tolerance on average but not calibrated","date":"2026-11-07T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 7: realistic bearing error \u2014 the frozen fused tracker still beats the frozen IMM, but T2 alone is more robust","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/08/phase-7"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 6: the fused tracker\'s uncertainty, recalibrated, is inconclusive at G7","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/06/phase-6"}},"content":"G7 on seed 215 was inconclusive: the fused tracker\'s 50% range interval covered 48.3%, and its\\nconfidence interval straddled the 48% floor. The rules allow one re-score on a larger fresh seed.\\nThe owner chose to run it. Decision 34 fixed the details before the data existed:\\n\\n- **What stayed the same:** the tracker, the reference, the frozen models, the multipliers and the\\n  rules.\\n- **What changed:** a fresh seed, 216, and a test split of 110,000 scenarios instead of 40,000.\\n  That is large enough to resolve a coverage of about 48.3% against the floor.\\n- **The forecast:** about an even chance of passing.\\n\\n## Result: pass\\n\\n| Coverage on seed 216 (184,667 G5 rows) | 50%                    | 90%   |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ----- |\\n| Range (log)                            | **48.6%** [48.3, 48.9] | 89.9% |\\n| Course                                 | 50.5%                  | 90.3% |\\n| Speed                                  | 49.4%                  | 89.9% |\\n\\nAll nine gated checks pass. The fused tracker\'s 90% intervals are narrower than the IMM\'s: 0.73 \xd7\\nfor range, 0.94 \xd7 for course and 0.89 \xd7 for speed. This was the one re-score, so the verdict is\\nfinal.\\n\\n## What the pass does and does not show\\n\\nFive independent reviewers agreed that the pass is correct. One recomputed every value with code\\nof its own, and another re-ran the gate. All five also agreed on how far the claim goes.\\n\\n- **Range intervals run about 1.5 points short.** At 50%, the two seeds together give about 48.5%\\n  [48.3, 48.8]. That is inside the \xb12-point tolerance but not centred on 50%. The pass rests on\\n  the tolerance. The reference IMM, fitted on the same validation data, lands on 50%. So reusing\\n  validation data does not explain the fused tracker\'s shortfall.\\n- **The intervals cover within tolerance only on the pooled mix of rows.** Range at 50% covers:\\n\\n  | Rows                                       | Coverage |\\n  | ------------------------------------------ | -------- |\\n  | Settled, before any manoeuvre (quiet legs) | 67%      |\\n  | Settled, after a manoeuvre                 | 39%      |\\n  | At zig + 5 min                             | 61%      |\\n  | At zig + 20 min                            | 40%      |\\n\\n  Seed 215 showed the same pattern within a point.\\n\\n- **Course and speed intervals are often uninformative.**\\n  - 17% of course 90% intervals span the whole circle, and the median course 90% interval is\\n    188\xb0 wide. The course width ratio of 0.94 therefore compares two mostly vague intervals.\\n  - 27% of speed 90% intervals reach below zero.\\n- **This was a second look.** Over both seeds, the chance of a false pass is about 5% at most, not\\n  2.5%. A smaller re-score would probably have been inconclusive again.\\n- **The pass judges these frozen multipliers.** The 0.3-point margin is smaller than the\\n  multipliers\' own sampling error. So the pass does not show that the recalibration procedure works\\n  in general.\\n\\n## Phase 6 in one line\\n\\nOn average over the G5 rows, the fused tracker\'s recalibrated intervals cover within the\\ntolerance, with range about 1.5 points short, and they are tighter than the IMM\'s. Within a regime they do not: the intervals are too wide\\non quiet legs and too narrow after a manoeuvre, and its course intervals are often vacuous.\\nConditional calibration, for example a multiplier that depends on time since the last manoeuvre,\\nwould be a new design. The owner decides on the follow-on.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261106-p6v-g7/` (verdict, regimes, panel review); decision\\n`experiments/20261106-p6v-rescore-decision/`; spec 018."},{"id":"/2026/11/06/phase-6","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/06/phase-6","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-06-phase-6.md","title":"Phase 6: the fused tracker\'s uncertainty, recalibrated, is inconclusive at G7","description":"C.6\'s second follow-on asks whether the tracker that passed G5 can say how sure it is. The fused","date":"2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.01,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 6: the fused tracker\'s uncertainty, recalibrated, is inconclusive at G7","date":"2026-11-06T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 6 re-score: G7 passes, with coverage within tolerance on average but not calibrated","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/07/phase-6-rescore"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 5: frozen models do not beat a CUSUM at detecting manoeuvres","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/05/phase-5"}},"content":"C.6\'s second follow-on asks whether the tracker that passed G5 can say how sure it is. The fused\\ntracker is the frozen IMM with T2\'s range fused in.\\n\\nThe owner chose two things:\\n\\n- **Calibrate the fused tracker\'s stated uncertainty**, without training.\\n- **The test: calibrated and sharper.** Its 50% and 90% intervals for range, course and speed must\\n  cover within \xb12 points of nominal, and be narrower than the reference IMM\'s after the same\\n  recalibration.\\n\\n## What was done\\n\\n- **Stated sds.** Both trackers now write them, from their covariance by the delta method. They\\n  are badly miscalibrated as stated: typical errors are a fraction of the stated sd, with heavy\\n  tails.\\n- **Recalibration, post hoc.** One pair of multipliers per output, fitted on validation, scales\\n  the sd into 50% and 90% intervals. A two-fold check on validation passed, and decision 33 froze\\n  the multipliers before seed 215 was generated for scoring.\\n\\n## Result: inconclusive\\n\\n| Coverage on seed 215 (G5 rows) | 50%                    | 90%   |\\n| ------------------------------ | ---------------------- | ----- |\\n| Range (log)                    | **48.3%** [47.8, 48.8] | 89.8% |\\n| Course                         | 50.5%                  | 90.2% |\\n| Speed                          | 49.3%                  | 89.6% |\\n\\nThe fused tracker\'s intervals are narrower than the IMM\'s: 0.73 \xd7 for range, 0.95 \xd7 for course and\\n0.89 \xd7 for speed. Eight of the nine checks pass. Range at 50% falls just short: its interval\\nstraddles the 48% floor.\\n\\n## What the panel added\\n\\nFive independent reviewers agreed the verdict is right, and reproduced it byte for byte. They also\\nnarrowed what can be claimed:\\n\\n- **The intervals are calibrated only on the pooled mix of rows.** On quiet straight legs before\\n  any manoeuvre they are far too wide: range 50% covers 67%. After a manoeuvre they are too narrow:\\n  38%. One multiplier cannot fix that shape.\\n- **Course and speed intervals are often uninformative.** 17% of course 90% intervals span the\\n  whole circle, and 27% of speed 90% intervals reach below zero.\\n- **The shortfall looks systematic, not noise.** The likely cause is that T2\'s training epoch, the\\n  fusion settings and the multipliers were all chosen on the same validation split.\\n- **The rules were the agent\'s.** The owner did not get to review them before the decision record.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nThe rules allow one re-score on a larger fresh seed. It would probably land near 48.3% again, so a\\npass would mean the range coverage just reaches the floor, not that the tracker is well calibrated.\\nThe owner decides whether to run it.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261106-p6u-g7/`; spec 018."},{"id":"/2026/11/05/phase-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/05/phase-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-05-phase-5.md","title":"Phase 5: frozen models do not beat a CUSUM at detecting manoeuvres","description":"G5 passed in phases 4b and 4c. SRD Annex C \xa7C.6 therefore lists incremental follow-ons, and the","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.04,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 5: frozen models do not beat a CUSUM at detecting manoeuvres","date":"2026-11-05T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 6: the fused tracker\'s uncertainty, recalibrated, is inconclusive at G7","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/06/phase-6"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 4c: one track, a tracker that listens to a network\'s range","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/02/phase-4c"}},"content":"G5 passed in phases 4b and 4c. SRD Annex C \xa7C.6 therefore lists incremental follow-ons, and the\\nfirst is **manoeuvre detection, judged against a CUSUM on the reference tracker\'s innovations**.\\n\\nThe owner chose two things for this attempt:\\n\\n- **No training.** The detector is built from models that already exist: the frozen recurrent\\n  tracker T2, and the reference IMM, which now uses decision 32\'s guarded initialisation.\\n- **The test.** Detection delay, with every detector\'s threshold set for the same false-alarm\\n  rate: one alarm per 2 h of quiet tracking.\\n\\n## What was compared\\n\\n- **The baseline.** A CUSUM on the IMM\'s innovations, tuned on validation from 12 declared\\n  settings to favour it.\\n  - **Clipping.** The innovations are heavy-tailed: a few huge values otherwise hold an alarm for\\n    hours. So both the variance kind and the mean-shift kind are clipped.\\n  - **The winner.** The two-sided mean-shift CUSUM, since a turn biases the bearings to one side.\\n- **The learned detectors.** Two candidates, declared in advance:\\n  - T2\'s course disagreement with the IMM;\\n  - T2\'s own course change over the last minute.\\n\\n## Result: it stopped at validation\\n\\nAt 0.5 false alarms per hour, on 7,479 course changes in seed-211 validation:\\n\\n|                               | Detected within 5 min |\\n| ----------------------------- | --------------------- |\\n| CUSUM (mean shift)            | **28.2%**             |\\n| T2\'s course change over 1 min | **19.2%**             |\\n| T2 against the IMM            | 5.9%                  |\\n\\n- **The learned detector is not carried.** It is 9 points behind, with interval [\u221210.2, \u22127.7]\\n  pp, so it was not taken to a test split, and fresh seed 215 was never generated.\\n- **The delay check could not be judged.** Every detector misses more than half the course\\n  changes at this rate, so the median delays are infinite. That rule was the agent\'s, written\\n  before the data; any later attempt should declare a delay statistic defined at its operating\\n  point.\\n\\n## Reported, not used: a crossover\\n\\nThe same detectors were also calibrated at looser rates:\\n\\n| False alarms per hour | 0.5   | 1     | 2     | 5     |\\n| --------------------- | ----- | ----- | ----- | ----- |\\n| CUSUM                 | 28.2% | 38.0% | 47.8% | 62.6% |\\n| T2\'s course change    | 19.2% | 39.0% | 58.1% | 71.7% |\\n\\nFrom about 1 false alarm per hour, T2\'s course change catches more manoeuvres in time, though about\\n20\u201340 s later among those both catch. Moving the operating point after seeing this would choose the\\nrule from the result, so it stays a reported observation.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nThe owner decides. Annex C\'s other follow-ons remain:\\n\\n- calibration of the learned uncertainty;\\n- realistic bearing error.\\n\\nRecord: `experiments/20261105-p5-research/validation.md`; spec 017."},{"id":"/2026/11/02/phase-4c","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/02/phase-4c","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-02-phase-4c.md","title":"Phase 4c: one track, a tracker that listens to a network\'s range","description":"Phase 4b\'s hybrid passed G5, but it was two estimators stitched together: T2\'s range beside the","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.62,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 4c: one track, a tracker that listens to a network\'s range","date":"2026-11-02T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 5: frozen models do not beat a CUSUM at detecting manoeuvres","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/05/phase-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 4: an end-to-end learned tracker wins overall, loses on straight legs","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/01/phase-4"}},"content":"Phase 4b\'s hybrid passed G5, but it was two estimators stitched together: T2\'s range beside the\\nIMM\'s velocity. Phase 4c keeps **one filter**. The frozen IMM takes the frozen network T2\'s range\\nas an extra measurement, weighted by T2\'s own stated uncertainty and inflated four-fold, once a\\nminute. Nothing was trained.\\n\\n**It passed G5** on a fresh 40,000-scenario test split (seed 214), decided by the agent review\\npanel:\\n\\n| Fused tracker / IMM, 97.5% interval | Limit | Result            |\\n| ----------------------------------- | ----- | ----------------- |\\n| Range, all gate rows                | < 1   | 0.82 [0.81, 0.84] |\\n| Course, all gate rows               | < 1   | 0.95 [0.94, 0.96] |\\n| Range, settled rows                 | < 1.1 | 0.86 [0.84, 0.89] |\\n| Course, settled rows                | < 1.1 | 0.95 [0.93, 0.97] |\\n| Speed, settled rows                 | < 1.1 | 0.90 [0.87, 0.92] |\\n\\nRecords: the\\n[gate record](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261102-p4f-g5/g5.md) and the\\n[panel review](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261102-p4f-g5/review.md).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What improved on phase 4b\\n\\n- **One state.** Range, course and speed come from one filter. Projected forward, its bearing stays\\n  as close to the truth as the IMM\'s own (1.02\xb0 after 5 min against 1.06\xb0), where the hybrid\'s\\n  drifted to 1.49\xb0.\\n- **Settled rows improved because of estimation, not copying.** Settled range, course and speed are\\n  all better than the IMM\'s and the hybrid\'s. The hybrid\'s settled velocity was mostly the IMM\'s.\\n- **The hybrid\'s worst case is gone.** Before any target manoeuvre the hybrid\'s range was 1.43 \xd7 the\\n  IMM\'s. The fused tracker\'s is 1.03 \xd7 [0.99, 1.07]: parity.\\n\\n## What it does not show\\n\\n- **It does not beat the IMM before a target manoeuvres.** On validation it seemed to (0.94 \xd7), but\\n  on the fresh seed it only matches it. The gains come after manoeuvres: 0.74 \xd7 the IMM\'s range\\n  error there.\\n- **The EKF and batch least squares are still better before any manoeuvre.** Against the EKF it\\n  would fail settled course (1.41 \xd7). Overall it beats the EKF by a wide margin (range 0.80 \xd7,\\n  course 0.78 \xd7).\\n- **The hybrid is still better on overall course** (fused 1.04 \xd7 the hybrid).\\n- **The settings were chosen in-sample**, from 18 variants on validation. The decision record says\\n  12; the panel found the error after scoring, and it is corrected around the frozen record.\\n  Only the fresh seed decides, and it did.\\n- **Its uncertainty is not calibrated.** Like the IMM\'s, its covariance is too wide on typical rows\\n  and too narrow in a small tail.\\n- **It is not a learned tracker.** It is a filter that listens to a frozen network. Phases 4b and 4c\\n  use the same T2 and IMM, so they are not independent replications. Both show that T2\'s range helps\\n  this IMM. Neither shows that a network alone tracks better.\\n\\n## The long view\\n\\nAfter G0 to G5 and three phase-4 attempts:\\n\\n- **What networks add.** A learned range estimate is genuinely useful to a tuned tracker, mostly\\n  after the target manoeuvres. Folded into the filter as a measurement, it gives one consistent track\\n  that beats the tuned IMM on every G5 check.\\n- **What they do not do.** On clean, straight-leg geometry the classical tools (an EKF, a batch fit)\\n  remain better.\\n\\nReal or recorded data are still the untested step."},{"id":"/2026/11/01/phase-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/01/phase-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-phase-4.md","title":"Phase 4: an end-to-end learned tracker wins overall, loses on straight legs","description":"Phase 4 asked whether a learned tracker, trained end to end, could beat a tuned IMM tracker on","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.88,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 4: an end-to-end learned tracker wins overall, loses on straight legs","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 4c: one track, a tracker that listens to a network\'s range","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/02/phase-4c"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 4b: a hybrid passes G5 \u2014 a learned range with a tuned tracker\'s velocity","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/01/phase-4b"}},"content":"Phase 4 asked whether a learned tracker, trained end to end, could beat a tuned IMM tracker on\\ntargets that manoeuvre up to three times. Two trackers were trained from scratch and compared\\nwith the frozen reference on the same validation split:\\n\\n- **T1**, a learned-gain filter: Kalman-style physics with a gain computed by a small recurrent\\n  network;\\n- **T2**, a pure recurrent tracker (2 \xd7 128 LSTM) that also states its own uncertainty.\\n\\n**Neither reached the test.** The pre-registered rule needs every gated check to pass on\\nvalidation.\\n\\n| Median error, ratio to the IMM | Limit | T2       | T1   |\\n| ------------------------------ | ----- | -------- | ---- |\\n| Range, all gate rows           | < 1   | **0.85** | 2.18 |\\n| Course, all gate rows          | < 1   | **0.84** | 1.32 |\\n| Range, settled rows            | < 1.1 | **0.97** | 2.80 |\\n| Course, settled rows           | < 1.1 | 1.44     | 2.12 |\\n| Speed, settled rows            | < 1.1 | 1.66     | 2.30 |\\n\\n\\"Settled\\" rows are at least five minutes after the target\'s last manoeuvre. Both of T2\'s\\nfailures have their whole 97.5% interval above the limit, so the fresh test split (seed 212) was\\nnever scored.\\n\\nRecord: the\\n[validation record](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261031-p4-val/validation.md).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\n- **Data.** Generator 0.5.0 gives the target 0\u20133 course changes and, in 30% of scenarios, a speed\\n  change, in 20\u201360 min windows.\\n- **Reference.** An IMM (constant velocity plus coordinated turn), tuned on validation over all\\n  gate rows and frozen. Its median range error there is 9.2%.\\n- **Gate rows.** \u03c3 = 1\xb0 bearings at 5, 10, 15 and 20 min after ownship\'s zig.\\n- **Forecast,** recorded before training: T1 carried with probability about 35%, T2 about 10%,\\n  G5 passed about 20%.\\n\\n## What happened\\n\\n**T2 is the best estimator yet on manoeuvring targets.**\\n\\n- **Range.** 7.8% median against the IMM\'s 9.2%. The lead grows with time and with each turn: at\\n  20 min after the zig, 10.8% against 16.6%.\\n- **Course.** After three turns, 34\xb0 against the IMM\'s 46\xb0.\\n- **Gross errors.** 3.5 percentage points fewer than the IMM.\\n- **Its stated uncertainty is honest.** 71% of its errors fall within one stated standard\\n  deviation (68% expected) and 95% within two (95% expected).\\n\\n**On straight legs it still loses.** On settled rows its speed error is 2.16 kt against the\\nIMM\'s 1.30 kt, and its course error 10.7\xb0 against 7.4\xb0. Before the first manoeuvre the gap is\\nwidest: 2.0 kt against 0.7 kt.\\n\\n**T1 never learned range.** Its range error is 19\u201322% at every gate time and does not fall after\\nownship\'s zig, as a working tracker\'s does.\\n\\n- **Why.** Its gain is expressed along and across the line of sight. The cross term is kept\\n  positive, so the bearing loop is stable at any range. The range term has to change sign with\\n  the geometry, and the network did not learn that from what it saw.\\n- **The first run diverged in epoch 0.** It was fixed before any result was seen, and the fix is\\n  recorded as an amendment to the decision.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **This is the third time the same pattern has appeared.** Phases 3, 3b and now 4 each found a\\n  network that beats a tuned tracker after manoeuvres and gives accuracy back when the target\\n  holds course. More turns per scenario moved the overall checks in the network\'s favour; they\\n  did not touch the straight-leg deficit.\\n- **The settled gap is mostly speed.** A Kalman filter on a straight leg gets velocity almost for\\n  free from a long, consistent bearing history. The networks have the same history but do not\\n  match it, even with a speed loss weight of 4.\\n- **A stronger straight-leg reference exists.** Batch least squares reaches 6.7% settled range,\\n  better than both the IMM and T2. The settled checks compare against a reference that is not the\\n  best achievable there.\\n- **The gate asked for dominance, not a trade.** On a mix of manoeuvring and straight targets, T2\\n  is better overall and worse when settled. Whether that trade is useful is an operational\\n  question that G5 deliberately did not answer.\\n- **Training was slower and less robust than planned.** There were four restarts: a diverging\\n  filter, a 2 GiB file limit, a refused cross-commit resume and a lost CI runner. None changed a\\n  result. T2\'s fresh run reproduced its first run\'s epochs to the last digit.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nSRD Annex C leaves the follow-on to the owner if G5 is not passed. Options include:\\n\\n- **A hybrid.** T2\'s manoeuvre advantage switched in only when it reports high uncertainty or a\\n  manoeuvre, with the tracker otherwise, as G3\'s hybrid did for classical estimates.\\n- **A narrower question.** Manoeuvre detection and recovery only, where the network already wins.\\n- **Stopping here** and writing up the programme.\\n\\nReal or recorded data stay deferred."},{"id":"/2026/11/01/phase-4b","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/01/phase-4b","source":"@site/blog/2026-11-01-phase-4b.md","title":"Phase 4b: a hybrid passes G5 \u2014 a learned range with a tuned tracker\'s velocity","description":"Phase 4\'s recurrent tracker T2 beat a tuned IMM tracker overall but lost on straight legs, so it","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.63,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 4b: a hybrid passes G5 \u2014 a learned range with a tuned tracker\'s velocity","date":"2026-11-01T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 4: an end-to-end learned tracker wins overall, loses on straight legs","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/01/phase-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 3b: two hypotheses fix the course, not the speed","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/30/phase-3b"}},"content":"Phase 4\'s recurrent tracker T2 beat a tuned IMM tracker overall but lost on straight legs, so it\\ndid not reach the test. The owner chose a follow-on: a **hybrid** of the two frozen estimators,\\njudged by G5 unchanged. The hybrid takes T2\'s range always, and T2\'s course and speed only where the\\ntwo estimators\' courses differ by more than 45\xb0. Elsewhere it takes the IMM\'s.\\n\\n**It passed**, on a fresh 40,000-scenario test split (seed 213), decided by the agent review panel\\nin two rounds:\\n\\n| Hybrid / IMM, 97.5% interval | Limit | Result            |\\n| ---------------------------- | ----- | ----------------- |\\n| Range, all gate rows         | < 1   | 0.86 [0.84, 0.88] |\\n| Course, all gate rows        | < 1   | 0.90 [0.89, 0.92] |\\n| Range, settled rows          | < 1.1 | 1.02 [0.99, 1.06] |\\n| Course, settled rows         | < 1.1 | 0.98 [0.97, 0.99] |\\n| Speed, settled rows          | < 1.1 | 0.99 [0.99, 1.00] |\\n\\nIt also makes 3.4 percentage points fewer gross range errors than the IMM.\\n\\nRecords: the\\n[gate record](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261101-p4h-g5/g5.md) and the\\n[panel review](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261101-p4h-g5/review.md).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## How it was set up\\n\\n- **Nothing was trained.** Both estimators were frozen: the IMM since decision 29, T2 since phase 4.\\n  `tma-g5` checked T2\'s weights by file digest.\\n- **Why a course disagreement.** The IMM lags a manoeuvre by minutes, and while it lags its course\\n  disagrees with T2\'s. A disagreement above \u03b8 is therefore a sign that T2\'s velocity is the better one.\\n- **Choosing \u03b8.** \u03b8 = 45\xb0 was chosen on validation by a criterion stated in advance: the largest\\n  smallest margin over the five checks. Every \u03b8 from 25\xb0 to 90\xb0 passed there, so 45\xb0 sits on a\\n  plateau, not a spike.\\n- **Freezing it.** The decision, \u03b8 and the test seed were committed and pushed before the test split\\n  was scored.\\n- **Forecast:** an 85% chance of passing.\\n- **No sign of over-fitting.** The three checks that depend on \u03b8 moved by less than 0.01 between\\n  validation and test.\\n\\n## What it does not show\\n\\nThe panel insisted on these, and they matter as much as the pass.\\n\\n- **It is a hybrid, not a learned tracker.** On its own, T2 still fails the straight-leg checks (phase\\n  4). The result shows that a learned estimator adds value beside a tuned tracker, as G3\'s hybrid\\n  did for classical least squares.\\n- **It is three numbers per row, not a track.** Range comes from one estimator and velocity from\\n  another, so together they do not satisfy either one\'s bearing-rate relation. Measured afterwards,\\n  the cost is small but real: on settled rows, projected 5 min ahead, its bearing is a median of 1.5\xb0\\n  off, against 1.0\xb0 for the IMM and 1.6\xb0 for T2 alone.\\n- **Before a target manoeuvres, its range is worse.** On settled rows before any manoeuvre, the\\n  hybrid\'s range error is 6.0% against the IMM\'s 4.1% (1.47 \xd7). After a manoeuvre it is 10.3% against\\n  15.2% (0.68 \xd7). The pooled settled check passes only because the second group carries it, even\\n  though the first group is 55% of settled rows. For a target holding course, the commonest case, a\\n  tuned filter is better.\\n- **Against the EKF it would fail two settled checks:** course 1.44 \xd7 and speed 1.21 \xd7. The IMM was the\\n  pre-registered reference, chosen on overall range, so the gate stands. But \\"better than a tuned\\n  tracker on straight legs\\" is not shown. Batch least squares is also better on settled range: 6.6%\\n  against 7.6%.\\n- **The settled velocity checks pass largely by construction.** The hybrid uses the IMM\'s velocity\\n  on most rows.\\n- **The rule family was chosen after seeing phase 4\'s result.** The fresh seed controls the choice\\n  of \u03b8, not the choice of family.\\n\\n## The long view\\n\\nThe programme\'s question was whether a network trained on simulated bearings could give usable\\nrange, course and speed. After G0 to G5, the answer is consistent.\\n\\n- **Where networks win.** They are better where tracking is hardest: after target manoeuvres, and\\n  late in long engagements. In range they reach about 0.7 \xd7 a tuned tracker after a manoeuvre.\\n- **Where they lose.** On clean geometry, a target holding course after an ownship zig, a tuned\\n  filter or a batch fit is better, especially on speed.\\n- **What works.** Combining them works, as a hybrid that takes each one\'s strength. A network alone\\n  does not.\\n\\nReal or recorded data remain the untested step."},{"id":"/2026/10/30/phase-3b","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/30/phase-3b","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-30-phase-3b.md","title":"Phase 3b: two hypotheses fix the course, not the speed","description":"Phase 3 found a network that beats a tuned IMM tracker after a target turns, but that averages","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.02,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 3b: two hypotheses fix the course, not the speed","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 4b: a hybrid passes G5 \u2014 a learned range with a tuned tracker\'s velocity","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/11/01/phase-4b"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 4 plan: two learned trackers, judged on manoeuvring targets","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/30/phase-4-plan"}},"content":"Phase 3 found a network that beats a tuned IMM tracker after a target turns, but that averages\\n\\"turned\\" and \\"not turned\\" on targets that hold course. Phase 3b gave the network a way out. It\\noutputs two estimates and the probability that the target has turned, and reports the more\\nprobable estimate.\\n\\n**It half worked.** On the same validation split:\\n\\n| Measure                           | Phase 3b    | Phase 3a | IMM tracker | Limit         |\\n| --------------------------------- | ----------- | -------- | ----------- | ------------- |\\n| After a turn: median range error  | 10.3%       | 10.5%    | 13.6%       | below the IMM |\\n| After a turn: median course error | 14.5\xb0       | 14.4\xb0    | 24.1\xb0       | below the IMM |\\n| Held course: course error         | 6.1\xb0        | 11.1\xb0    | 5.0\xb0        | 10\xb0           |\\n| Held course: speed error          | **1.15 kt** | 1.60 kt  | 0.71 kt     | 1 kt          |\\n\\nThe speed interval, [1.05, 1.25] kt, lies wholly above the limit. The pre-registered rule\\ntherefore stopped phase 3b at validation, and its test split was never scored. Under SRD Annex\\nB, this was the last phase-3 attempt.\\n\\nRecords: [results](/docs/results/phase-3) and the\\n[validation record](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261030-p3b-val/validation.md).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\nOnly the network\'s output changed. The data, the frozen IMM reference and the gate were all as\\nin phase 3.\\n\\n- **The network** gives a held-course estimate, a turned estimate, and P(turned).\\n- **The flag\'s training.** The training label is the truth: has the target\'s course changed yet?\\n- **The forecast** in the decision record, written before training, was held-course course about\\n  6\xb0 and speed about 0.95 kt (0.8\u20131.2), with a 40% chance of reaching the test split.\\n\\n## What happened\\n\\n- **Course: fixed.** Held-course course error fell from 11.1\xb0 to 6.1\xb0, as forecast. It no longer\\n  grows with time.\\n- **After a turn: unchanged.** The network still beats the IMM by about a quarter on range and\\n  40% on course.\\n- **Speed: not fixed.** Speed fell from 1.60 to 1.15 kt, inside the forecast band but above the\\n  limit.\\n\\nThe flag explains the speed failure:\\n\\n- **It detects real turns well.** The median delay is 70 s, and it misses 0.5%.\\n- **It also false-alarms.** On 29\u201331% of held-course rows five and ten minutes after the zig,\\n  it calls \\"turned\\". In this data, those minutes are when targets usually start to turn.\\n- **A false alarm is costly.** The turned estimate is biased slow, so each one costs about\\n  1.8 kt.\\n- **Even without false alarms, speed sits at the limit.** Where the network says \\"held\\", its\\n  speed error is 1.00 kt, so a perfect flag would leave it there too. The IMM manages 0.71 kt and\\n  batch least squares 0.31 kt.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **Switching moves the failure rather than removing it.** An averaged output blurs every\\n  estimate a little. A switched output is sharp, but sometimes sharply wrong: held-course course\\n  P90 rose from 34\xb0 to 51\xb0.\\n- **No threshold tuning.** A stricter threshold than 0.5 would cut false alarms but slow\\n  detection. Choosing it on this split would make validation a training signal, so it was not\\n  done.\\n- **Two changes at once.** The output form and its supervision changed together, as declared,\\n  so this result cannot separate them.\\n- **The finding is consistent.** Two different network designs beat a tuned tracker after a\\n  turn, and both give back accuracy on targets that hold course. Neither dominates the tracker,\\n  and dominating it is what gate G4 asks.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nSRD Annex B \xa7B.5\'s second branch applies, as it did for phase 3:\\n\\n1. publish the negative result, which this post does;\\n2. the owner then decides separately whether an end-to-end learned tracker is a new project.\\n\\nReal or recorded data stay deferred either way."},{"id":"/2026/10/30/phase-4-plan","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/30/phase-4-plan","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-30-phase-4-plan.md","title":"Phase 4 plan: two learned trackers, judged on manoeuvring targets","description":"Phases 3 and 3b ended the same way. A network beats a tuned IMM tracker after a target turns,","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.24,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 4 plan: two learned trackers, judged on manoeuvring targets","date":"2026-10-30T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 3b: two hypotheses fix the course, not the speed","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/30/phase-3b"},"nextItem":{"title":"Phase 3: better than the tracker after a turn, worse when there is none","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/29/phase-3"}},"content":"Phases 3 and 3b ended the same way. A network beats a tuned IMM tracker after a target turns,\\nbut gives that back on targets that hold course. SRD Annex B left the next step to the owner,\\nwho chose the separate project it names: **an end-to-end learned tracker**.\\n\\n[SRD Annex C](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/docs/SRD-annex-C.md) fixes the design\\nbefore any code is written.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Two trackers, one gate\\n\\n- **A learned-gain filter** (KalmanNet-style). The bearing and motion physics stay explicit, and a\\n  recurrent network learns the gain: how fast to adapt when the bearings stop fitting a straight\\n  track. The hope is filter accuracy on straight legs and network agility after a manoeuvre.\\n- **A pure recurrent tracker.** The LSTM family we already have, outputting position, velocity\\n  and its own uncertainty at every step. It is the simpler bet, carrying the known weakness: on\\n  straight-leg speed it has stayed at about 3 \xd7 classical for six iterations.\\n\\nBoth are trained from scratch on the same data and judged on the same rows.\\n\\n## A harder problem\\n\\nGenerator 0.5.0 gives the target 0\u20133 course changes and, in 30% of scenarios, a speed change.\\nManoeuvres become the norm, which is where a constant-velocity model should struggle most. The\\nIMM and EKF are re-tuned for it, and the better one is frozen before training.\\n\\n## Gate G5\\n\\nA tracker passes if:\\n\\n- **it is better overall:** its median range and course error, over rows at 5, 10, 15 and\\n  20 minutes after the zig, is below the reference\'s;\\n- **and it does not lose much once things settle:** on rows more than 5 minutes after the last\\n  manoeuvre, it is within 1.1 \xd7 the reference on range, course and speed.\\n\\nThe second condition stops phase 3\'s trade, winning after turns by losing on straight legs, from\\ncounting as a pass.\\n\\nThe statistics are stricter than before:\\n\\n- **The bootstrap resamples whole scenarios**, because one scenario contributes several rows.\\n- **Each tracker\'s intervals are 97.5%,** because two trackers get a shot at the gate.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **What follows a failure is not fixed in advance.** If G5 fails, the owner decides what follows\\n  then. That is weaker than Annex B, where both branches were set before the result. The write-up\\n  will say so.\\n- **The speed bar is demanding.** The settled-speed check asks the trackers to come within 10% of\\n  a reference that is already about 2 \xd7 least squares on straight-leg speed.\\n- **The learned-gain filter is a risk.** Backpropagation through about 300 filter steps may be\\n  slow or unstable in tfjs. Its cost is measured before the decision record commits to it."},{"id":"/2026/10/29/phase-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/29/phase-3","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-29-phase-3.md","title":"Phase 3: better than the tracker after a turn, worse when there is none","description":"Phase 3 asked the question the earlier gates could not: does a learned estimator have an edge","date":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":2.74,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Phase 3: better than the tracker after a turn, worse when there is none","date":"2026-10-29T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 4 plan: two learned trackers, judged on manoeuvring targets","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/30/phase-4-plan"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 5: a hybrid passes G3; the network adds robustness, not accuracy","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/28/iteration-5"}},"content":"Phase 3 asked the question the earlier gates could not: does a learned estimator have an edge\\nwhere the classical model breaks, when the target turns?\\n\\n**After a turn, yes.** Five minutes after the turn ends, on the validation split:\\n\\n| Measure             | Network | Tuned IMM tracker | Ratio            |\\n| ------------------- | ------- | ----------------- | ---------------- |\\n| Median range error  | 10.5%   | 13.6%             | 0.77             |\\n| Median course error | 14.4\xb0   | 24.1\xb0             | 0.60             |\\n| Gross errors        |         |                   | 5.7 points fewer |\\n\\nThe IMM was the better of two tracking filters tuned on the same data.\\n\\n**On targets that never turn, no.** Its course error is 11.1\xb0 and its speed error 1.60 kt, both\\npast G2\'s limits (10\xb0, 1 kt). The pre-registered rule therefore stopped the phase at validation.\\nThe test split was never scored.\\n\\nRecords: [results](/docs/results/phase-3) and the\\n[validation record](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261029-p3a-val/validation.md).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What was tested\\n\\n- **The data.** The target makes one course change of 30\u2013120\xb0 in 80% of scenarios (generator\\n  0.4.0).\\n- **The reference.** We tuned two tracking filters on validation, a single Cartesian EKF and an\\n  IMM (constant velocity plus coordinated turn). We froze the better one: the IMM.\\n- **The network.** It is the iteration-4 LSTM design, unchanged, trained from scratch on the new\\n  data. Its decision record was committed before training. It forecast about a 30% chance of a\\n  pass, with range the likely failure.\\n\\n## What happened\\n\\n- **After a turn.** The network beats the tracker on range, course, speed and gross errors. Its\\n  lead holds whether or not ownship manoeuvres after the turn. The network has learned something\\n  the tracker\'s model does not hold, for example that the target\'s speed is unchanged through a\\n  turn.\\n- **On held course.** It regresses. It reports speeds 1.2 kt too low on median, and its course\\n  error rises to 11\xb0 five minutes after the zig, which in this data is exactly when targets\\n  usually turn.\\n\\nA network trained to minimise squared error reports an average when it cannot tell whether a\\nturn has started. An averaged velocity is shorter, and its direction is hedged. This is the\\ndilemma every tracking filter faces, between following a turn quickly and staying steady on a\\nstraight leg, now inside the network.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **The forecast was wrong in an informative way.** We expected range to be the obstacle. Range\\n  and course both cleared easily; the held-course control failed instead.\\n- **The control is strict but fair.** It asks the network not to regress below G2\'s absolute bar\\n  on the 20% of targets that hold course. A tracker that is tuned for turns pays a similar price:\\n  the IMM is 5.0\xb0 and 0.71 kt on those rows, against batch least squares\' 1.2\xb0 and 0.31 kt.\\n- **One validation look.** This is not a test-split result, and the test split remains unseen.\\n  The margins on the failing checks are wide: both intervals lie wholly beyond the limits.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nSRD Annex B \xa7B.5 fixed this branch in advance:\\n\\n1. publish the result;\\n2. then decide separately whether a different design is a new project.\\n\\nThe new design could be an end-to-end tracker, a network that outputs \\"turned or not\\" rather\\nthan an average, or a hybrid that uses the network only after a detected turn. That decision is\\nthe owner\'s."},{"id":"/2026/10/28/iteration-5","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/28/iteration-5","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-28-iteration-5.md","title":"Iteration 5: a hybrid passes G3; the network adds robustness, not accuracy","description":"G2 left the models usable but about 3 \xd7 worse than the classical least-squares solver. G3","date":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.23,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 5: a hybrid passes G3; the network adds robustness, not accuracy","date":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Phase 3: better than the tracker after a turn, worse when there is none","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/29/phase-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 4: velocity outputs pass G2, still 3 \xd7 classical","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/25/iteration-4"}},"content":"G2 left the models usable but about 3 \xd7 worse than the classical least-squares solver. G3\\nasks for parity: within 1.2 \xd7 classical on every metric, and a lower gross-error rate.\\n\\nIteration 5 trained nothing. It combined the two estimators: classical\'s estimate, except where\\nclassical fails or the network disagrees by more than 50% on range, where the network\'s is used.\\n\\n**G3 passes for that hybrid** on a fresh test set of 20,000 scenarios (seed 207):\\n\\n- **Parity.** Every median and 90th percentile of range, course and speed is within 0.97\u20131.02 \xd7\\n  classical.\\n- **Gross errors.** Five minutes after the zig they fall from 1.19% to 0.57% (\u22120.62 pp\\n  [\u22120.82, \u22120.42]).\\n- **Validation agreed.** It forecast 1.25% \u2192 0.55% on seed 205.\\n\\nRecords: [`experiments/20261028-i5-g3/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261028-i5-g3).\\nFigures: [iteration-5 results](/docs/results/iteration-5).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## How we got here\\n\\nThe owner first chose a model lever for G3: a per-leg steady-track fit as an extra input (Annex A\\nB.2.3). Three pieces of research reshaped the plan before anything was trained:\\n\\n1. **The per-leg fit doesn\'t help where it matters.** On a long first leg it measures the\\n   range-rate ratio well, about 28% error. At the gate points the current leg is only about four\\n   minutes old, and the same quantity is pure noise: 37\u201364 /h of error against a true value near\\n   1 /h. Classical does better because it fits all the legs together.\\n2. **Starting the solver from the network changes nothing.** It gave classical\'s answer in 99.9%\\n   of rows. Classical\'s gross errors are not bad starting points. They are cases where the\\n   least-squares optimum itself is far from the truth: near-unobservable geometry, where any start\\n   converges to the same wrong answer. In 91% of those rows the network was still within 50% of\\n   the true range.\\n3. **So the hybrid has to sometimes overrule least squares.** The disagreement switch does,\\n   without a tuned parameter. Its 50% threshold is the gross-error bound itself.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **It is a hybrid result.** The network alone is still 0.956 kt on speed against classical\'s\\n  0.307 kt. Nothing here says a network matches classical.\\n- **No accuracy gain.** The hybrid\'s medians are about 1% _worse_ than classical\'s on course and\\n  speed: measurable, and immaterial against 1.2. What it adds is fewer gross errors: the 1.4% of\\n  rows where it switches.\\n- **The switch aims at the metric that is gated.** The threshold equals the gross-error\\n  definition. That was fixed in the decision record before seed 207 existed, but it means the\\n  lower gross rate is partly by design.\\n- **It costs something at short range.** At zig + 300 s the switch fixes 82 classical gross\\n  errors and causes 21. Those 21 are mostly under 4 km, where classical is sharp and the network\'s\\n  relative range error is large.\\n- **The gain is early only.** Ten minutes after the zig the gross rates are 0.17% against 0.14%:\\n  no evidence of a difference. The \\"lower gross-error rate\\" is shown at the pre-registered\\n  five-minute point only.\\n- **It grows with noise.** At \u03c3 = 2\xb0 gross errors fall from 4.63% to 1.80%. That is the case SRD\\n  \xa75 calls interesting for ML over classical methods.\\n- **Still simulation.** The target holds course and speed (SRD \xa73), and the data are mock.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nAll four gates are passed: G0, G1, G2, and G3 for the hybrid. The proof of concept has its\\nanswer.\\n\\n- **The network alone** extracts usable range, course and speed, but is about 3 \xd7 worse than\\n  classical.\\n- **As a fallback** it makes least squares more robust in near-unobservable geometry, without\\n  costing accuracy.\\n\\nSRD \xa75 says G3 defines the bar for the next phase. Natural next steps are target manoeuvres\\n(where classical\'s constant-velocity model breaks) and an EKF reference. Both are the owner\'s\\nchoice."},{"id":"/2026/10/25/iteration-4","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/25/iteration-4","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-25-iteration-4.md","title":"Iteration 4: velocity outputs pass G2, still 3 \xd7 classical","description":"Iteration 4 changed the models, not the problem. It kept iteration 3\'s three-leg engagements, made","date":"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.94,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 4: velocity outputs pass G2, still 3 \xd7 classical","date":"2026-10-25T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 5: a hybrid passes G3; the network adds robustness, not accuracy","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/28/iteration-5"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 3: a second zig helps everyone, classical most","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/22/iteration-3"}},"content":"Iteration 4 changed the models, not the problem. It kept iteration 3\'s three-leg engagements, made\\nthe models output a velocity (vx, vy) instead of a course and a speed, and added the derived\\nfeatures (bearing rate, its validity flag, the bearing-line intercept).\\n\\n**G2 passes.** The seed-205 test split was inconclusive at 0.981 kt [0.940, 1.031]. The rules\\nallow one re-score on a larger fresh-seed test set, and on seed 206, with 100,000 scenarios and the\\nsame frozen models, every check passes.\\n\\n- **Speed passes.** The instantaneous LSTM reaches 0.939 kt [0.926, 0.951] at zig + 600 s on the\\n  \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice, against 1 kt. The BiLSTM reaches 0.740 kt.\\n- **Range and course pass easily:** 7.3% and 4.7\xb0 for the LSTM, 5.3% and 3.1\xb0 for the BiLSTM.\\n- **Better than iteration 3 on the same rows.** Range, course and speed are 16\u201327% better.\\n- **Still about 3 \xd7 classical.** Classical reaches 0.32 kt on the same rows. The LSTM is\\n  2.1 \xd7 classical on range, 3.5 \xd7 on course and 2.9 \xd7 on speed.\\n\\nRecords: [`experiments/20261025-i4-g2-rescore/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261025-i4-g2-rescore)\\nand [`experiments/20261024-i4-g2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261024-i4-g2).\\nFigures: [iteration-4 results](/docs/results/iteration-4).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\nIteration 3 stopped at 1.10 kt. Its LSTM shrank speed estimates towards the middle of the range:\\nslow targets were overestimated and fast ones underestimated, while classical was unbiased.\\n\\n- **Velocity components.** They give one smooth target for course and speed, with no wrap-around\\n  and no out-of-range values. Course and speed are derived from the vector.\\n- **Not enough on its own.** Research R1 showed that removing the bias alone would only reach\\n  about 1.01 kt.\\n- **Derived features.** The owner therefore added them, to target the remaining scatter.\\n\\n- **Models.** The iteration-3 configuration (two stacked 128-unit recurrent layers), from scratch\\n  on seed 205.\\n- **Outputs.** Log range plus vx and vy divided by the top of the target speed range. The loss\\n  weights each component 4\xd7.\\n- **Pre-registered.** The decision record, forecast and validation rule were committed before\\n  training. Validation gave 0.94 kt [0.91, 0.98] and carried the pair to test.\\n\\n## Seed-206 test split (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                     | Range, zig+300 s | Course, zig+300 s | Speed, zig+600 s            |\\n| ----------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------- | --------------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                  | < 20%            | < 10\xb0             | < 1 kt                      |\\n| lstm-instantaneous            | **7.26%**        | **4.70\xb0**         | **0.939 kt** [0.926, 0.951] |\\n| bilstm-time-late              | **5.30%**        | **3.13\xb0**         | **0.740 kt**                |\\n| iteration-3 models (reported) | 8.7% / 7.2%      | 6.24\xb0 / 4.31\xb0     | 1.12 / 1.00 kt              |\\n| classical                     | 3.40%            | 1.36\xb0             | 0.319 kt                    |\\n| naive                         | 39.9%            | 89.8\xb0             | 4.23 kt                     |\\n\\nn = 49,574 at zig + 300 s and 32,266 at zig + 600 s.\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **A narrow pass.** 52.6% of the LSTM\'s speed estimates are within 1 kt, and the P90 is 2.9 kt\\n  (classical 1.9 kt). The interval\'s upper bound is well below the threshold, so the pass is not\\n  in statistical doubt. The margin itself is small.\\n- **The bias is still there.** The LSTM\'s median signed speed error is \u22120.33 kt, and \u22121.5 kt for\\n  18\u201320 kt targets: fast targets are called slow. The re-score\'s forecast predicted this. Removing\\n  the bias would leave 0.86 kt; most of the error is scatter.\\n- **Short range is hardest.** Under 2 km the LSTM is 0.97 kt against 0.72\u20130.78 kt at 2\u20138 km, and\\n  about 20 \xd7 classical. Near closest approach the bearing rate changes fast, and the 3-minute\\n  trailing rate lags it.\\n- **It took two looks.** Seed 205 was inconclusive and seed 206 passed. The rule allowed exactly\\n  this. Allowing two looks roughly doubles the false-pass rate at a true median of exactly 1 kt,\\n  from about 1.8% to about 3.7%. The two points differ by 0.04 kt, about 1.8 standard errors. Most\\n  of the difference is chance: seed 205\'s validation split also gave 0.94.\\n- **Two levers at once.** The record cannot say whether velocity outputs or derived features moved\\n  speed.\\n- **The re-score needed four attempts.** Cloud containers were reclaimed mid-run three times, on a\\n  run of about 4.5\u20135.3 hours against containers that live about 4\u20134.6 hours. The scoring is\\n  deterministic and every input is bound by digest. Every attempt produced byte-identical files,\\n  and the result was fixed before anyone saw it. The last attempt reused the verified prediction\\n  files (`tma-g2 --reuse-predictions`).\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nG2 is passed: the models give usable range, course and speed on the problem as posed. G3 asks for\\nparity with classical, which is about 3 \xd7 better on speed and course. That is a much larger step\\nthan any lever so far has given. Under SRD \xa77, the plan moves to G3, and the owner chooses whether\\nand how to pursue it."},{"id":"/2026/10/22/iteration-3","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/22/iteration-3","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-22-iteration-3.md","title":"Iteration 3: a second zig helps everyone, classical most","description":"Iteration 3 changed the problem, not the models. After the owner\'s realism check in the viewer,","date":"2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.28,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 3: a second zig helps everyone, classical most","date":"2026-10-22T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 4: velocity outputs pass G2, still 3 \xd7 classical","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/25/iteration-4"},"nextItem":{"title":"The engagement viewer: single cases behind the G2 numbers","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/17/engagement-viewer"}},"content":"Iteration 3 changed the problem, not the models. After the owner\'s realism check in the viewer,\\nownship now zigs twice, and both turns cross the line of sight. The 2c models were trained from\\nscratch on the new engagements. Speed improved by almost half, but not enough: the iteration\\nstopped at validation under its pre-registered rule, and the test split was not scored.\\n\\n- **Speed fails the rule.** The instantaneous LSTM reaches 1.10 kt [1.06, 1.16] at zig + 600 s on\\n  the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice. The rule needed an interval reaching below 1 kt. The forecast, recorded before\\n  training, was 1.1\u20131.3 kt.\\n- **The BiLSTM gets closest of any model so far:** 1.02 kt [0.98, 1.05]. It is reported, not gated.\\n- **Range and course pass easily:** 8.8% and 6.2\xb0 for the LSTM, 7.2% and 4.4\xb0 for the BiLSTM.\\n- **Classical gains more.** On the same scenarios it reaches 0.34 kt, so the LSTM is now 3.3 \xd7\\n  classical on speed, against 2.4 \xd7 in iteration 2c.\\n\\nRecord: [`experiments/20261021-i3a-val/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261021-i3a-val).\\nFigures: [iteration-3 results](/docs/results/iteration-3).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\nThe owner watched iteration-2c engagements in the [viewer](/viewer) and judged that, with both\\nplatforms on steady legs, there was too little information. An operator without a steady solution\\nwould zig again, traditionally across towards the contact. The seed-203 gate data agreed in part:\\nturns that crossed the line of sight gave the best classical solutions, and an earlier zig mainly\\nshortened the first leg (spec 007 research R1).\\n\\n- **Generator 0.3.0.** A second zig 3\u20135 minutes after leg 2 starts. Each turn puts the target\\n  30\u201390\xb0 on the other bow, by the shorter turn. The first zig keeps its timing.\\n- **Models.** The 2c configuration (two stacked 128-unit recurrent layers, speed weighted 4\xd7 in the\\n  loss), from scratch on a fresh dataset, seed 204.\\n- **Pre-registered rule.** The decision record was committed before training. The pair would go to\\n  the test split only if the LSTM\'s speed interval on validation reached below 1 kt.\\n\\n## Seed-204 validation split (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                       | Range, zig+300 s | Course, zig+300 s | Speed, zig+600 s     |\\n| ------------------------------- | ---------------- | ----------------- | -------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                    | < 20%            | < 10\xb0             | < 1 kt               |\\n| lstm-instantaneous              | **8.8%**         | **6.19\xb0**         | 1.10 kt [1.06, 1.16] |\\n| bilstm-time-late                | **7.2%**         | **4.36\xb0**         | 1.02 kt [0.98, 1.05] |\\n| 2c models (out of distribution) | 11.4% / 17.7%    | 6.53\xb0 / 7.72\xb0     | 2.01 / 1.93 kt       |\\n| classical                       | 3.4%             | 1.43\xb0             | 0.34 kt              |\\n| naive                           | 40.2%            | 89.7\xb0             | 4.23 kt              |\\n\\n## Reading it critically\\n\\n- **The lever worked on the problem, not on the gap.** Every estimator improved. Classical halved\\n  its speed error (0.61 \u2192 0.34 kt), as the forecast said it would. The models improved too, but\\n  the ratio to classical grew on every measure: 3.3 \xd7 on speed, 2.6 \xd7 on range and 4.3 \xd7 on course.\\n  The extra information went mostly to the estimator that already uses it well.\\n- **The \\"improvement\\" against 2c is weak evidence.** The 2c models were trained on the two-leg\\n  problem; on these engagements they degrade to about 2 kt. Beating them shows that training on the\\n  new data helped, nothing more. The rule said so in advance.\\n- **This is an easier problem.** Large turns (median 143\xb0) and longer windows make TMA easier for\\n  everyone. A pass here would have been a pass on the three-leg problem, not on iteration 2\'s.\\n- **The BiLSTM near-miss costs.** It took 35 runner-hours over three dispatches to stop by\\n  patience. Its interval straddles the threshold, but it is not the gated model, and the pair\\n  moves together.\\n\\n## What next\\n\\nFour attempts have now failed on speed, with the gap to classical between 2.4 \xd7 and 3.3 \xd7. Changing\\nthe engagements again looks unpromising: it helps classical at least as much. The remaining Annex A\\nlevers change the models instead: output parameterisation, derived features, training changes or\\ncapacity (B.2.2\u2013B.2.5). The next one is the owner\'s choice, pre-registered with a fresh seed."},{"id":"/2026/10/17/engagement-viewer","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/17/engagement-viewer","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-17-engagement-viewer.md","title":"The engagement viewer: single cases behind the G2 numbers","description":"The gates judged the models on medians over thousands of scenarios. The new","date":"2026-10-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":3.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"The engagement viewer: single cases behind the G2 numbers","date":"2026-10-17T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 3: a second zig helps everyone, classical most","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/22/iteration-3"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 2c: G2 failed on speed; the project stops at G2","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/17/iteration-2c"}},"content":"The gates judged the models on medians over thousands of scenarios. The new\\n[engagement viewer](/viewer) shows one scenario at a time: the plan, both platforms\' course and\\nspeed, the bearings, and each gate\'s carried models beside the classical solver and the naive\\nbaseline. It is the first step of SRD Annex A. Its first use is the owner\'s realism check of the\\ngenerator, which comes before any change to the training data.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What it does\\n\\n- **Regenerates scenarios in the browser** from their seed with the pipeline\'s own generator. No\\n  scenario files ship. A test checks the browser path is byte-identical to Node.\\n- **Runs the models in the browser** with tfjs on the CPU backend. The same encoding and decoding\\n  the pipeline uses feed it, and a test finds it within float32 tolerance of `tfjs-node`.\\n- **Three modes.** Truth shows the engagement. Instantaneous shows the causal LSTM\'s estimate at\\n  a \\"now\\" cursor, from the bearings up to then. Time-late shows the bidirectional LSTM\'s estimate\\n  at every sample of the window.\\n- **Every gate\'s carried pair**: iteration 1 (G1 pass) and iterations 2, 2b and 2c (G2 fail).\\n- **The URL holds the view**, so every link below reopens exactly what is described.\\n\\nThe 2c BiLSTM runs in about 1 s for one scenario in headless Chromium; the LSTM in about 0.5 s.\\nThe eight models total 4.5 MB.\\n\\nHow it works: [the viewer](/docs/algorithms/viewer).\\n\\n## A typical engagement\\n\\n[Seed 203, scenario 97612, instantaneous, zig + 10 min](/viewer?src=s203-test&i=97612&mode=instantaneous&t=1690&pair=i2c).\\nThe 2c LSTM sits near its own median errors here.\\n\\n![The typical engagement in instantaneous mode](/img/blog/viewer-typical.jpg)\\n\\n| At zig + 10 min | Range error | Course error | Speed error |\\n| --------------- | ----------- | ------------ | ----------- |\\n| 2c LSTM         | 11%         | 6\xb0           | 1.6 kt      |\\n| Classical       | 3%          | 0\xb0           | 0.9 kt      |\\n| Naive           | 41%         | 51\xb0          | 6.6 kt      |\\n\\nBefore the zig the model still answers, but range is unobservable from bearings alone. The viewer\\ngreys that period out. Here the LSTM\'s range sits well short of the truth before the zig and\\nreaches it only after the turn.\\n\\n[The same scenario in time-late mode](/viewer?src=s203-test&i=97612&mode=time-late&t=1690&pair=i2c)\\nshows something the medians hide. The BiLSTM\'s estimated positions form a jagged path rather than\\na straight track. Each sample\'s estimate comes from its own output row, and nothing ties them to\\none constant-velocity solution. The classical batch solution is one straight track by\\nconstruction. In this case the BiLSTM\'s post-zig median speed error, 3.0 kt, is worse than the\\ninstantaneous LSTM\'s 1.8 kt.\\n\\n![The typical engagement in time-late mode](/img/blog/viewer-typical-tl.jpg)\\n\\n## The largest speed error\\n\\n[Seed 203, scenario 95484, instantaneous, zig + 10 min](/viewer?src=s203-test&i=95484&mode=instantaneous&t=1980&pair=i2c)\\nhas the 2c LSTM\'s largest speed error at zig + 10 min among the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 scenarios: 10.9 kt.\\n\\n![The largest speed error](/img/blog/viewer-worst.jpg)\\n\\nThe geometry is hard, not broken. The target starts 2.5 km away and opens at 17.3 kt on a course\\nwithin about 10\xb0 of the line of sight. The bearing barely moves, so speed along the line of sight\\nis weakly observable. The LSTM answers 6.5 kt, pulled towards typical training speeds. The\\nclassical solver struggles too: 13 of its 101 post-zig solutions fail to converge, and its\\nmedian speed error is 6.5 kt. One case cannot say how often this happens; it shows one way the\\nmodels fail.\\n\\n## A held-out engagement beyond the training ranges\\n\\n[Seed 201 held-out, scenario 102983, time-late, zig + 5 min](/viewer?src=s201-heldout&i=102983&mode=time-late&t=1300&pair=i2c)\\nstarts at 25.8 km. Training ranges ran from 2 to 20 km. The BiLSTM\'s range estimate starts near\\n18 km against a true 25 km: in this case the model does not reach ranges beyond those it was\\ntrained on. The classical solver has no such limit.\\n\\n![The held-out engagement in time-late mode](/img/blog/viewer-far.jpg)\\n\\n| Post-zig medians | Range error | Course error | Speed error |\\n| ---------------- | ----------- | ------------ | ----------- |\\n| 2c BiLSTM        | 31%         | 34\xb0          | 6.3 kt      |\\n| Classical        | 12%         | 3.5\xb0         | 0.85 kt     |\\n| Naive            | 55%         | 53\xb0          | 7.7 kt      |\\n\\n## What comes next\\n\\nThe owner now runs the realism check in Annex A \xa7A.5: do the tracks, turns, speeds and bearing\\nnoise look like real engagements? Its record (\xa7A.7) decides which Part B levers, such as larger\\nor more ownship course changes, are worth pre-registering on fresh seeds from 204 onward. Nothing\\nis trained until then.\\n\\nThese three cases illustrate; they do not measure. Frequencies come from the gate experiments,\\nnot from cases picked in a viewer."},{"id":"/2026/10/17/iteration-2c","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/17/iteration-2c","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-17-iteration-2c.md","title":"Iteration 2c: G2 failed on speed; the project stops at G2","description":"Iteration 2c was the last attempt the SRD allows under G2. It combined the two levers that each","date":"2026-10-17T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":8.76,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 2c: G2 failed on speed; the project stops at G2","date":"2026-10-17T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"The engagement viewer: single cases behind the G2 numbers","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/17/engagement-viewer"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/15/iteration-2b"}},"content":"Iteration 2c was the last attempt the SRD allows under G2. It combined the two levers that each\\nmoved speed on validation, a speed-weighted loss and twice the model capacity, and judged G2 on a\\nthird fresh test split. Range and course are the best of the project. Speed is not within reach.\\n\\n**Verdict: G2 failed** (agent review panel, round 2, no rejections). Under SRD \xa77 the project stops at G2:\\n**no-go.**\\n\\n- **Range passes** for both models: 13.5% [13.0, 13.9] instantaneous and 12.1% [11.7, 12.6]\\n  time-late at zig + 300 s on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice, against 20%.\\n- **Course passes for both:** 7.36\xb0 [7.05, 7.70] and 6.12\xb0 [5.87, 6.41], against 10\xb0.\\n- **Speed fails for both:** 1.47 kt [1.37, 1.52] and 1.53 kt [1.47, 1.61] at zig + 600 s, against\\n  1 kt. Only 37% and 33% of scenarios are under 1 kt. Classical least squares reaches 0.61 kt on\\n  the same scenarios, at the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound.\\n\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261016-i2d-g2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261016-i2d-g2).\\nCurves and slices: [iteration-2c results](/docs/results/iteration-2c).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\nIteration 2b showed that weighting speed 4\xd7 in the loss moved speed by 0.3 kt; the\\n[capacity scan](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261014-i2s-scan) showed\\nthat a two-layer 128-unit LSTM reaches the 96-unit model\'s plateau in half the epochs. SRD \xa77 was\\namended on 2026-10-15, after 2b\'s validation result and before its test scoring, to allow one\\nfurther iteration combining the two, then stop (decisions 18\u201320, spec 005).\\n\\n- **Model.** Two stacked recurrent layers of 128 units (203k parameters for the LSTM, 538k for the\\n  BiLSTM, against 41k and 123k before), trained from scratch; the CNN and MLP controls at their\\n  sizes.\\n- **Loss.** Speed weighted 4\xd7, as in 2b.\\n- **Fresh test split.** Seed 203, pinned by content digest in the plan; seeds 201 and 202 decided\\n  the earlier attempts; all three are now reserved in the plan checker.\\n- **Cost.** The BiLSTM took 27.5 runner-hours over five chained jobs in two runs; the LSTM\\n  12.6 hours. Per-epoch cost rose 1.4\xd7 and 2.2\xd7 against 2b.\\n\\nThe decision record, with a forecast of 1.4\u20131.6 kt, was committed four hours before training\\nstarted. Validation gave 1.42 / 1.52 kt; the test split gave 1.47 / 1.53 kt.\\n\\n## Results on the seed-203 test split (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                         | Range, zig+300 s       | Course, zig+300 s      | Speed, zig+600 s     |\\n| --------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                      | < 20%                  | < 10\xb0                  | < 1 kt               |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (2c)           | **13.5%** [13.0, 13.9] | **7.36\xb0** [7.05, 7.70] | 1.47 kt [1.37, 1.52] |\\n| bilstm-time-late (2c)             | **12.1%** [11.7, 12.6] | **6.12\xb0** [5.87, 6.41] | 1.53 kt [1.47, 1.61] |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (2b, reported) | 15.3%                  | 8.27\xb0                  | 1.78 kt              |\\n| bilstm-time-late (2b, reported)   | 15.8%                  | 9.12\xb0                  | 1.77 kt              |\\n| classical                         | 8.8%                   | 3.41\xb0                  | 0.61 kt              |\\n| naive                             | 37.2%                  | 90.5\xb0                  | 4.44 kt              |\\n\\nn = 4,689 scenarios at zig + 300 s (93% of the slice) and 3,038 at zig + 600 s (60%).\\n\\nThe repeated G1 checks: both models at 0.35\u20130.39 \xd7 naive on range (pass); controls beaten;\\nshuffled labels at 1.027 \xd7 naive (pass). The time-late model is, for the first time, better than\\nthe instantaneous one on range and course, and worse on speed; its \\"not worse\\" check fails at\\nthree late points without an interval (the paired intervals contain the slack), the deficit late,\\nlargest at \u03c3 = 2\xb0 and worst near the window end.\\n\\n## The trend, and why it stops here\\n\\n| Attempt                 | Change                                      | Speed, instantaneous | Speed, time-late |\\n| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------- | ---------------- |\\n| Iteration 2 (seed 201)  | G1 models, longer training                  | 2.09 kt              | 2.38 kt          |\\n| Iteration 2b (seed 202) | speed weight 4, continued                   | 1.77 kt              | 1.77 kt          |\\n| Iteration 2c (seed 203) | 2 \xd7 128 units, from scratch, speed weight 4 | 1.47 kt              | 1.53 kt          |\\n\\nEach step moved the instantaneous model\'s speed by about 0.3 kt (the time-late model\'s by 0.6 kt\\nthen 0.24 kt), and the 2c-over-2b gain is real: on the same seed-203\\nrows the paired difference is \u22120.31 kt [\u22120.41, \u22120.23] for the instantaneous model and \u22120.24 kt\\n[\u22120.32, \u22120.15] for the time-late one. But a pass needs the whole interval below 1 kt, a median of\\nabout 0.93 kt: a further 37%, more than both steps combined. The panel\'s statistics reviewer put\\nit this way, and the panel adopted it as the no-go statement:\\n\\n> On the fresh seed-203 test split the instantaneous model\'s median speed error is 1.47 kt\\n> (95% CI 1.37\u20131.52) and the time-late model\'s 1.53 kt (1.47\u20131.61): 12 and 14 standard errors\\n> above the 1 kt threshold, with only 37% / 33% of scenarios under 1 kt; the interval would\\n> still exclude 1 kt with as few as about 80 scenarios (56 for the time-late model). Range and\\n> course pass with wide margins. Each iteration moved speed by about 0.3 kt and the result\\n> matched validation and the pre-recorded forecast; reaching the gate would need a further 37%,\\n> larger than both steps combined, from a capacity lever the scan already showed flat (at 30\\n> epochs with speed weight 1: 192 units 2.25 kt, 2 \xd7 128 units 2.24 kt) and a loss-weight lever\\n> that bought 15%. Classical least squares reaches 0.61 kt on the same rows,\\n> at the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound: the information is in the data; this family of models does not\\n> extract it. Caveats: three different test splits across iterations (split-to-split differences\\n> at most 1.4 standard errors), one training seed per model, two levers tried.\\n\\n## What the models did and did not learn\\n\\nThe panel\'s geometry and numerics reviewers agree on the shape of the failure.\\n\\n- **Speed error does not follow the geometry.** By true range at zig + 600 s the model\'s error is\\n  1.2\u20132.0 kt in every band, where classical\'s falls from 1.6 kt at 20\u201340 km to 0.1 kt under 2 km.\\n  By bearing rate it is flat at 1.3\u20131.8 kt against classical\'s 0.4\u20130.9, and the ratio to\\n  classical is largest, 3.7\xd7, in the best-observed band (above 1\xb0/min). By range the ratio\\n  reaches 16\xd7 below 3 km. Against the per-scenario Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound the models\' median\\n  error-to-bound ratio is 1.30, where classical\'s is 0.66 and an efficient estimator\'s 0.67, so\\n  about twice an efficient estimator in the median and nine times the bound below 3 km.\\n- **The gains were uniform shifts.** The 2c improvement over 2b is \u22120.2 to \u22120.3 kt in every range\\n  band and at every time. By true speed it came from the bands away from the population mean\\n  (3\u20139 kt and 15\u201318 kt); 9\u201315 kt and 18\u201320 kt moved by under 0.1 kt. That is eased regression to\\n  the mean (slope of prediction on truth 0.65 \u2192 0.71, about 16% less shrinkage); scatter fell by\\n  only 5\u20139% (2.23 \u2192 2.11 kt). The term that has to move is the one that moved least.\\n- **The floor is scatter.** With every speed-dependent bias removed the median error would still\\n  be 1.35 kt. A bidirectional model that sees the whole window did no better on speed than the\\n  causal one.\\n- **Outside the training range band there is no speed information.** On held-out scenarios\\n  starting at 20\u201330 km the models\' speed error is 3.4 kt at zig + 600 s, 0.8 \xd7 the naive prior,\\n  and classical\'s 3.6 kt is no better. Five minutes after the zig the models beat classical on all\\n  three quantities there, because classical fails to converge on a quarter of those scenarios; ten\\n  minutes after, classical leads on range and course.\\n- **Range and course are a different story.** They follow the bearing-line geometry the network\\n  reads, and five minutes after the zig the time-late model is ahead of classical beyond 10 km\\n  and at \u03c3 = 2\xb0 (14.0% / 7.3\xb0 / 1.86 kt against 17.1% / 7.4\xb0 / 2.29 kt). That lead is robustness\\n  to non-convergence: classical fails to converge on 10\u201316% of those scenarios and is scored\\n  infinite there, while on the scenarios it does solve its course is the better one. Given ten\\n  minutes, classical leads everywhere. That early robustness is the project\'s positive result,\\n  and it is not what G2 asks.\\n\\nSpeed from bearings is second-order information: it lives in how the bearing rate changes as\\nownship manoeuvres. Classical least squares uses it explicitly; the networks, given raw bearings\\nand ownship states, learn a population prior refined by about two knots of scatter.\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers, two rounds. Round 1 returned approve-with-findings from all five with no blocker\\nor major finding; every reviewer reproduced the speed medians independently, the reproducibility\\nreviewer re-gated, re-evaluated and re-predicted byte for byte, and the red team found no path\\nfrom a pipeline error to the verdict. Round 2 approved with wording minors only; the\\ncorrections are in this post. Record:\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261016-i2d-g2/review.md).\\n\\nNineteen minor findings were answered or fixed. Two are worth repeating: the output scale for\\nspeed (ownship\'s 15 kt maximum) puts targets above 15 kt beyond the normalised range, where the\\nmodels shrink most (not saturation: the head is linear and predictions reach 19.9 kt), and this\\nlead was never tried; and the models were trained with one seed each,\\nso seed-to-seed variation in these medians is unmeasured.\\n\\n## What was not tried\\n\\nWithin the SRD\'s hyperparameter cap, three leads stayed on the table:\\n\\n1. Derived features (bearing rate and the bearing-line intercept), built in iteration 2 and never\\n   trained on.\\n2. Rescaling the speed target to unit variance, or a dedicated speed head.\\n3. A per-leg steady-track fit as an input or an auxiliary target, giving the network the\\n   second-order quantity it does not extract.\\n\\nNone of them is a lever the record shows moving speed, and the SRD\'s budget is spent. A follow-on\\nwould need a pre-registered validation result with the whole interval below about 0.9 kt from at\\nleast two training seeds before another test split is scored.\\n\\n## Where this leaves the question\\n\\nThe SRD asked whether a network trained on simulated bearings-only data can produce usable range,\\ncourse and speed. On range and course, yes, with intervals well inside the thresholds and, early in the track, better\\nrobustness to least squares\' non-convergence. On speed, no: three iterations\\nreduced the error from 2.1 to 1.5 kt against a 1 kt threshold that classical meets with margin.\\nThe gate is failed and the project stops at G2."},{"id":"/2026/10/15/iteration-2b","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/15/iteration-2b","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-15-iteration-2b.md","title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","description":"Iteration 2b asks one question: does weighting speed in the loss get the iteration-2 models to the","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":7.9,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","date":"2026-10-15T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 2c: G2 failed on speed; the project stops at G2","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/17/iteration-2c"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/14/iteration-2"}},"content":"Iteration 2b asks one question: does weighting speed in the loss get the iteration-2 models to the\\nG2 speed threshold? It changes nothing else, and judges G2 on a fresh test split.\\n\\n**Verdict: G2 failed** (agent review panel, round 2, no rejections).\\n\\n- **Range passes** for both models: 14.6% [14.1, 15.2] instantaneous and 15.8% [15.2, 16.2]\\n  time-late at zig + 300 s on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice, against 20%.\\n- **Course passes for both:** 8.22\xb0 [7.88, 8.54] and 8.66\xb0 [8.29, 9.03], against 10\xb0. The\\n  time-late course failure of iteration 2 is gone.\\n- **Speed fails for both, by less:** 1.77 kt [1.68, 1.83] and 1.77 kt [1.67, 1.86] at\\n  zig + 600 s, against 1 kt. The same weights before 2b, scored on the same scenarios, were at\\n  2.02 kt and 2.31 kt. Classical reaches 0.60 kt.\\n\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261015-i2c-g2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261015-i2c-g2).\\nCurves and slices: [iteration-2b results](/docs/results/iteration-2b).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed\\n\\nThe SRD allows one more round after a stalled G2. Iteration 2 left three leads (research R19):\\nderived features, speed in the loss, and the training budget. The user chose the loss (decisions\\n15\u201317 in spec 004):\\n\\n- **Loss weight.** The speed term of the loss is weighted 4\xd7 (`loss_weight_speed: 4`). Speed\\n  contributed about 15% of the loss before; now it is about 40%.\\n- **Continued, not restarted.** Every model (the two carried ones and the three controls) starts\\n  from its committed phase-B weights, to 220 epochs at most with patience 6. Because the loss\\n  changed, `tma-train` re-scores the best epoch under the new loss before it resumes; otherwise\\n  the old best, on the old scale, could never be beaten and the old weights would be kept.\\n- **A fresh test split.** The iteration-2 test split (seed 201) decided the first attempt and is\\n  never scored again. The gate is judged on the test split of seed 202, generated with the same\\n  count and ranges and pinned by content digest in the training plan. Train and validation are\\n  unchanged.\\n\\nThree tooling checks promised at the end of iteration 2 arrived with 2b and were on for this\\ngate: `tma-g2` checks the plan\'s content digests itself, refuses a phase decision that is not a\\ngit ancestor of the carried weights, and checks that every estimator was scored on the same rows,\\nnot just the same number of them. The [evaluation](/docs/evaluation) page describes them.\\n\\nThe carried set was fixed before training: the 2b models, unconditionally. Phase B is scored\\nbeside them on the fresh split and reported, never gated.\\n\\n## Results on the fresh test split (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                        | Range, zig+300 s       | Course, zig+300 s      | Speed, zig+600 s     |\\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                     | < 20%                  | < 10\xb0                  | < 1 kt               |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (2b)          | **14.6%** [14.1, 15.2] | **8.22\xb0** [7.88, 8.54] | 1.77 kt [1.68, 1.83] |\\n| bilstm-time-late (2b)            | **15.8%** [15.2, 16.2] | **8.66\xb0** [8.29, 9.03] | 1.77 kt [1.67, 1.86] |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (B, reported) | 16.5%                  | 9.20\xb0                  | 2.02 kt              |\\n| bilstm-time-late (B, reported)   | 19.2%                  | 10.36\xb0                 | 2.31 kt              |\\n| classical                        | 8.5%                   | 3.31\xb0                  | 0.60 kt              |\\n| naive                            | 37.9%                  | 90.4\xb0                  | 4.22 kt              |\\n\\nn = 4,682 scenarios at zig + 300 s (93% of the slice) and 2,973 at zig + 600 s (59%).\\n\\nPhase B\'s numbers here differ from the iteration-2 verdict (2.09 / 2.38 kt) because this is a\\ndifferent split; the intervals overlap.\\n\\n### The repeated G1 checks\\n\\n| Check                                  | Result                                   |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------- |\\n| instantaneous / naive < 0.5            | pass: 0.418 [0.405, 0.432]               |\\n| time-late / naive < 0.5                | pass: 0.430 [0.419, 0.443]               |\\n| time-late not worse than instantaneous | fail: 2 of 19 points (t = 840 and 960 s) |\\n| both models beat their controls        | pass                                     |\\n| shuffled-label model at naive          | pass: 1.000 \xd7                            |\\n\\nThe time-late model\'s knife-edge G1 fail from iteration 2 (0.514) is now a clear pass. Its\\ndeficit since G1 was a training deficit, not an architectural one: given the same budget it\\nmatches the instantaneous model on every check. The \\"not worse\\" check has no interval; the two\\nexceedances (ratios 1.055 and 1.056 against 1.05) have paired intervals that contain 1.05, so\\nthis fail is not distinguishable from a pass. What is real is the G1 caveat persisting: the\\ntime-late model is slightly worse than the instantaneous one at 17 of 19 points, not better.\\n\\n## What the speed weight did, and did not do\\n\\n- **Speed moved by 12\u201323%** (2.02 \u2192 1.77 kt; 2.31 \u2192 1.77 kt), and range and course improved with\\n  it. Nothing got worse.\\n- **It is still 1.8 kt against 1 kt**, with the whole interval above 1.67 kt. The models are\\n  about three times classical, which is at the Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound on these bearings: the panel\\n  recomputed the per-scenario bound on this split (median 1.05 kt for the bearings to\\n  zig + 600 s) and classical\'s error is 0.62 of it at the median, where an efficient estimator\\n  gives 0.67.\\n- **The gain is less scatter as much as less shrinkage.** The slope of predicted on true speed\\n  rose from 0.58 to 0.65 and the residual spread fell from 2.38 to 2.21 kt; correcting the\\n  shrinkage alone would not help (it would give 2.3 kt). Target speeds above ownship\'s 15 kt map\\n  to normalised targets above 1, where the shrinkage is worst; the output scaling was not changed.\\n- **The gap is where classical is near-perfect.** By true range at zig + 600 s (all noise):\\n\\n| True range         | Instantaneous | Time-late | Classical |\\n| ------------------ | ------------- | --------- | --------- |\\n| 2\u20135 km (n 731)     | 1.44 kt       | 1.43 kt   | 0.17 kt   |\\n| 5\u201310 km (n 2,219)  | 1.55 kt       | 1.53 kt   | 0.57 kt   |\\n| 10\u201320 km (n 2,568) | 2.12 kt       | 2.01 kt   | 1.51 kt   |\\n| 20\u201330 km (n 420)   | 2.98 kt       | 3.08 kt   | 3.54 kt   |\\n\\nClassical\'s 0.60 kt median is carried by the short ranges, where a few hundred metres of range\\nerror fixes speed to a fraction of a knot. The models are 1.4\u20131.5 kt there, flat across bands\\nthey resolve well, and only beyond 20 km do they beat classical on speed.\\n\\n- **The range advantage beyond 10 km is narrower than iteration 2 suggested.** At zig + 300 s\\n  the models are ahead at 10\u201320 km (14.2% against 18.6%, all noise) and at 20\u201330 km (24.1%\\n  against 27.4%), but the gap is carried by classical\'s non-converged tail (117 of 1,806 scenarios\\n  at 10\u201320 km on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice, scored as infinite error). On converged scenarios alone the two\\n  are level, and at zig + 600 s classical is better in every band.\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers again: TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics,\\nreproducibility, and red team. All five returned approve-with-findings in round 1, and none\\nrejected. Every one of them reproduced the speed medians independently, and none found a path\\nfrom a pipeline error to the verdict.\\n\\n**One latent major, fixed.** When a continuation changes the loss, `tma-train` re-scores the best\\nweights under the new loss before it resumes. It used that value in memory but never wrote it to\\nthe manifest, so a chained job (resuming with its optimizer state) compared new-loss epochs against\\nthe old-loss number. Had a continuation\'s first job ended before any epoch beat the re-scored\\nvalue, later jobs would have kept the old weights silently. It did not happen in this run (the\\nreviewer checked every job boundary in the CI logs), but it could have. The manifest now records\\nthe value a later epoch must beat and the source of a continuation, a chained-job test covers the\\ncase, and `tma-train --check` verifies that a finished continuation\'s kept weights are explained\\nby its record.\\n\\n**What else the panel established:**\\n\\n- The fresh split is fresh: per-scenario seeds derive from the dataset seed and index, a seed-201\\n  and seed-202 collision would need an index difference of 10\xb9\u2078, and no test scenario shares a\\n  seed or parameter set with any of the 100,000 seed-201 scenarios.\\n- The fail margin is 16\u201320 standard errors. Only 30% of scenarios have a speed error under 1 kt;\\n  for the interval merely to touch the threshold the test set would have to be about 28 scenarios.\\n- The re-scoring worked as specified: the reviewers recomputed the re-scored losses to five\\n  decimals and confirmed each model\'s best epoch was chosen under the new loss. The shuffled\\n  control\'s weights are unchanged from phase B because no epoch beat its re-scored loss, which is\\n  the right outcome for a control.\\n- Classical\'s non-converged scenarios (1.4%) are scored as infinite error and included; its\\n  0.60 kt is not flattered.\\n\\nNineteen minor findings were answered or fixed in two rounds, mostly record-keeping: the\\nmanifests of the committed 2b models do not say they are continuations (the facts are in the\\nreview), gate-deciding seeds are now reserved in the plan checker, and the write-up wording above\\nfollows the geometry reviewer\'s corrections.\\n\\nRecord:\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261015-i2c-g2/review.md).\\n\\n## Next\\n\\nSRD \xa77 was amended on 2026-10-15 to allow one further round, iteration 2c, and then stop. The\\ncapacity scan (research R20) showed that a two-layer 128-unit LSTM reaches the 96-unit model\'s\\nplateau in half the epochs. Iteration 2c trains that model from scratch with the speed weight, on\\nyet another fresh test split (seed 203). The decision record, with the forecast (1.4\u20131.6 kt) and\\nthe stopping rule, is committed before training:\\n[`experiments/20261015-i2d-decision/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261015-i2d-decision)."},{"id":"/2026/10/14/iteration-2","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/14/iteration-2","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-14-iteration-2.md","title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","description":"Iteration 2 asks whether the iteration-1 models give a useful estimate on realistic data: noisy","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":7.82,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","date":"2026-10-14T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 2b: speed-weighted loss, G2 failed again","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/15/iteration-2b"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/12/iteration-1"}},"content":"Iteration 2 asks whether the iteration-1 models give a _useful_ estimate on realistic data: noisy\\nbearings, dropouts and a rate-limited ownship turn.\\n\\n**Verdict: G2 failed** (agent review panel, round 2, no rejections).\\n\\n- **Range passes** for both models. Median range error at zig + 300 s on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice is\\n  16.5% [16.1, 17.1] instantaneous and 18.5% [17.7, 19.1] time-late, against a 20% threshold.\\n- **Course passes for the instantaneous model only:** 9.46\xb0 [9.02, 9.90]. The time-late model\\n  fails at 10.57\xb0 [10.16, 11.04], against 10\xb0.\\n- **Speed fails decisively for both:** 2.09 kt [2.00, 2.18] and 2.38 kt [2.28, 2.48] at\\n  zig + 600 s, against 1 kt. Classical reaches 0.62 kt on the same scenarios.\\n\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261014-i2-g2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261014-i2-g2).\\nCurves and slices: [iteration-2 results](/docs/results/iteration-2).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## What changed from iteration 1\\n\\n- **Noise and dropouts.**\\n  - Bearing noise \u03c3 \u2208 {1\xb0, 2\xb0}, half the scenarios each.\\n  - Dropouts drawn per sample, at 0\u201310% per scenario.\\n  - G2 is judged on the \u03c3 = 1\xb0 slice. The \u03c3 = 2\xb0 slice and the two dropout bands are reported.\\n- **A rate-limited turn.**\\n  - Ownship\'s zig is now flown at 0.5\u20133\xb0/s instead of instantaneously.\\n  - The zig time is the end of the turn, so \\"zig + 300 s\\" means five minutes after ownship is\\n    steady on its new leg.\\n  - This changed the generator (0.2.0), so G0 was re-run and passed again (panel rounds 1\u20133,\\n    [`20261013-g0-i2-r2`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261013-g0-i2-r2)).\\n- **Provenance.**\\n  - Every dataset header names the generator\'s commit.\\n  - Every model records the header of the data it was trained on.\\n  - `tma-train` refuses to resume on different features. `tma-g2` refuses to score the test\\n    split before the phase decision is committed.\\n- **Confidence intervals.**\\n  - Every gated median carries a seeded bootstrap 95% interval.\\n  - A check whose interval contains its threshold is [inconclusive](/docs/glossary), not a pass\\n    or fail.\\n\\n## Two training phases, one test score\\n\\nThe test split was protected by a two-phase rule, fixed before training (research R5).\\n\\n**Phase A** ran the G1 configurations unchanged: 60 epochs, patience 3, one seed per model. On\\nvalidation, the instantaneous model passed range (17.4%) and failed course (10.47\xb0) and speed\\n(2.24 kt), so phase A stalled. The decision record was committed before phase B started:\\n[`decision.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261013-i2a-val/decision.md).\\n\\n**Phase B** made one change, the training budget. Every model continued from its phase-A weights\\nto 150 epochs with patience 6. This was the user\'s choice over the pre-registered default\\n(derived features), and the record says what it tests: whether undertraining explains the gap.\\n\\nPhase B improved range (16.6%) and course (9.56\xb0) on validation, but not speed (2.12 kt). It was\\ncarried by the pre-declared rule: its range error was no worse than phase A\'s by more than phase\\nA\'s interval half-width.\\n\\nThe test split was then scored **once**, for phase B, with phase A beside it.\\n\\n## Results on test (\u03c3 = 1\xb0)\\n\\n| Estimator                        | Range, zig+300 s       | Course, zig+300 s      | Speed, zig+600 s     |\\n| -------------------------------- | ---------------------- | ---------------------- | -------------------- |\\n| G2 threshold                     | < 20%                  | < 10\xb0                  | < 1 kt               |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (B)           | **16.5%** [16.1, 17.1] | **9.46\xb0** [9.02, 9.90] | 2.09 kt [2.00, 2.18] |\\n| bilstm-time-late (B)             | **18.5%** [17.7, 19.1] | 10.57\xb0 [10.16, 11.04]  | 2.38 kt [2.28, 2.48] |\\n| lstm-instantaneous (A, reported) | 17.7%                  | 10.45\xb0                 | 2.26 kt              |\\n| bilstm-time-late (A, reported)   | 20.0%                  | 11.24\xb0                 | 2.64 kt              |\\n| classical                        | 8.8%                   | 3.42\xb0                  | 0.62 kt              |\\n| naive                            | 37.0%                  | 89.9\xb0                  | 4.25 kt              |\\n\\nn = 4,707 scenarios at zig + 300 s (93% of the slice) and 2,995 at zig + 600 s (59%).\\n\\n### The repeated G1 checks\\n\\nThese are judged on all rows.\\n\\n| Check                                  | Result                                      |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- |\\n| instantaneous / naive < 0.5            | pass: 0.472 [0.458, 0.484]                  |\\n| time-late / naive < 0.5                | fail: 0.514 [0.500, 0.529], on the boundary |\\n| time-late not worse than instantaneous | fail, as at G1                              |\\n| both models beat their controls        | pass                                        |\\n| shuffled-label model at naive          | pass: 1.015 \xd7                               |\\n\\nThe time-late fail sits on a knife-edge. The interval\'s lower bound is 0.50035, and with other\\nbootstrap seeds it falls between 0.4995 and 0.5006. It does not decide the gate.\\n\\n## Why speed fails\\n\\nThe panel ruled out the pipeline:\\n\\n- Three reviewers re-predicted phase B independently and got exactly 2.09 kt and 2.38 kt.\\n- The model\'s mean speed is right: 11.58 kt predicted against 11.69 kt true.\\n\\nThe errors come from shrinkage towards the mean instead:\\n\\n- The slope of predicted against true speed is about 0.57.\\n- Slow targets are over-estimated and fast ones under-estimated: errors are 2.6 kt at 0\u20135 kt and\\n  2.9 kt at 15\u201320 kt, against 1.5 kt at 10\u201315 kt.\\n\\nThe information is there. A per-scenario Cram\xe9r\u2013Rao bound on the actual bearings gives a median\\nachievable speed error of 0.64 kt, and classical reaches 0.62 kt. The models are about 3.3 times\\nthe bound.\\n\\nThree things point to how the models are trained, not to a ceiling of the approach:\\n\\n- **Speed gets little weight in the loss.** All four loss terms are weighted equally, and speed\\n  contributes about 15% of the loss.\\n- **Training stopped early.** In phase B the training loss was still falling (LSTM 0.400 \u2192 0.377\\n  over its last 14 epochs). Validation loss moved by about \xb13% from epoch to epoch, more than the\\n  per-epoch gain, so patience 6 ended the run.\\n- **More epochs barely moved speed.** The phase-B change improved range and course but not\\n  speed.\\n\\n## Where the models do well\\n\\nRange bands at zig + 300 s, all noise levels:\\n\\n| True range         | Instantaneous         | Classical          | Naive |\\n| ------------------ | --------------------- | ------------------ | ----- |\\n| 2\u20135 km (n 1,438)   | 20.0%                 | 5.3%               | 147%  |\\n| 5\u201310 km (n 3,486)  | 15.3%                 | 9.5%               | 23.0% |\\n| 10\u201320 km (n 3,728) | **16.7%**, gross 2.3% | 18.5%, gross 24.7% | 31.9% |\\n| 20\u201330 km (n 534)   | 31.4%                 | 28.7%              | 58.6% |\\n\\nShort ranges carry classical\'s advantage. Beyond 10 km the models match or beat it on range, and\\nfail grossly far less often. The same holds at \u03c3 = 2\xb0: the instantaneous model\'s range error\\nmoves only from 16.5% to 18.4%, while classical\'s doubles from 8.8% to 17.1%.\\n\\nOn held-out geometry, with initial range 20\u201330 km (outside training, reported only), the models\\nbeat classical on every median:\\n\\n| Estimator | Range      | Course    | Speed        |\\n| --------- | ---------- | --------- | ------------ |\\n| Models    | 43.8\u201344.8% | about 36\xb0 | about 3.6 kt |\\n| Classical | 48.8%      | 50.9\xb0     | 9.2 kt       |\\n\\n## What the panel found\\n\\nFive reviewers: TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics, reproducibility,\\nand red team. All five returned approve-with-findings in round 1, and none rejected.\\n\\n**Two major findings, both resolved:**\\n\\n- **Phase A\'s predictions overwrote phase B\'s on disk.** `tma-g2` wrote both phases\' prediction\\n  files under the same names. The verdict was unaffected, because it was scored in memory, but\\n  re-scoring from disk would have given phase A\'s numbers. Fixed: the other phase\'s files are now\\n  tagged.\\n- **The models were stopped by noise, not converged.** Accepted. The verdict is stated for this\\n  architecture, these inputs and this budget.\\n\\n**A gate-rule fix.** The G2 code failed a check whose interval straddled its threshold whenever\\nthe point estimate was at or above it. The spec says that case is inconclusive. The code now\\nfollows the spec, and a check passes only if its point estimate is also below the threshold.\\n\\nRe-gating the committed metrics with the new code gave identical checks, and the reproducibility\\nreviewer re-ran the whole gate byte for byte.\\n\\nRound 2 approved with only minor findings open. Record:\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261014-i2-g2/review.md).\\n\\n## What G2 cannot tell us\\n\\n- **The speed point covers 59% of scenarios.** Only windows that run at least ten minutes past\\n  the turn are scored at zig + 600 s, so the figure describes longer windows (decision 13). At\\n  zig + 300 s, where 93% are scored, speed is still 2.30 kt.\\n- **Medians hide the range dependence.** The range pass is not carried by short ranges, which are\\n  the models\' weak band. But a single median mixes a 10\u201320 km band where the models beat\\n  classical with a 2\u20135 km band where they are four times worse.\\n- **One seed per model** (decision 11). The intervals cover test-set sampling, not\\n  training-to-training variation.\\n- **The course pass is narrow.** Its interval\'s upper bound is 9.90\xb0 against a 10\xb0 threshold.\\n- **Targets never manoeuvre.** G2 says nothing about tracking a target\'s own zig.\\n- **Not done:** scoring the G1 models on a turn-only set (reported only, not gated). It is\\n  deferred to iteration 2b.\\n\\n## Next: iteration 2b\\n\\nThe SRD allows one more round after a stalled G2 (decision 5). The leads, in research R6, R18 and\\nR19:\\n\\n1. **Derived features.** Bearing rate and the bearing-line intercept, already built\\n   (`feature_set: derived`), give the network quantities classical TMA works from.\\n2. **Speed in the loss.** Weight it per term, or scale its target to unit variance.\\n3. **Per-leg steady-track fit.** Within each ownship leg, fit tan B(t) = (x\u2080 + v\u2093t)/(y\u2080 + v_yt)\\n   to the bearings. The fitted shape constrains relative velocity without needing range, and the\\n   residual flags a target manoeuvre.\\n4. **Budget and stopping.** A smoothed stopping criterion or a longer run, since training loss\\n   was still falling.\\n\\nBefore 2b scores a gate, three tooling fixes are due:\\n\\n- `tma-g2` checks the plan\'s content digests itself;\\n- the decision commit must be an ancestor of the weights\' commit;\\n- the same-rows check compares row identity, not just counts."},{"id":"/2026/10/12/iteration-1","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/12/iteration-1","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-12-iteration-1.md","title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","description":"Iteration 1 asks the question the whole project exists for: given simulated bearings and the","date":"2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":24.98,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","date":"2026-10-12T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 2: G2 failed on speed","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/14/iteration-2"},"nextItem":{"title":"Iteration 0: G0 passes \u2014 the pipeline is verified","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/11/g0-pipeline-verified"}},"content":"Iteration 1 asks the question the whole project exists for: given simulated bearings and the\\nownship track, can a small neural network estimate target range better than a guess from the\\nprior?\\n\\n**Verdict: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat.** Both model variants beat the naive baseline\\nby more than 2\xd7 at the gate point, with the whole 95% interval below 0.5: instantaneous 0.419\\n[0.407, 0.431], time-late 0.450 [0.438, 0.464] (medians 16.1% and 17.3%; naive 38.4%, classical\\n4.6%). The shuffled-label model scores at naive. The machine verdict is **inconclusive**, because\\nthe time-late model is measurably _worse_ than the instantaneous one (1.075 [1.046, 1.108] \xd7 at\\nzig + 300 s) although it sees more data; the agent review panel passed G1 under a rule fixed\\nbefore the deciding run (pre-registered rule 1, SRD \xa75) and recorded that as the caveat.\\nExperiment of record:\\n[`experiments/20261012-g1-r2/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261012-g1-r2).\\n\\nIt took two attempts. In **attempt 1** (`experiments/20261011-g1-r2/`) the time-late model was cut\\noff by a wall-clock cap after 25 of its 60 epochs and failed (0.551 [0.538, 0.565]). One\\npre-registered continuation of that model, to early stopping at 50 epochs, gave the pass. Full\\ncurves and slices: [iteration-1 results](/docs/results/iteration-1).\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## The question\\n\\nG1 ([SRD \xa75](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/docs/SRD.md#5-baselines-metrics-and-success-gates))\\npasses if, for **both** model variants, the median relative range error 5 minutes after the\\nownship zig is below half of the [naive baseline](/docs/algorithms/naive-baseline)\'s, on the same\\ntest scenarios. The naive baseline predicts the training-set mean range, course and speed, so a\\nmodel that beats it by 2\xd7 has learned something about the geometry, not just the prior.\\n\\nThe two variants answer different questions. The **instantaneous** model is causal: its estimate\\nat a given time uses only bearings up to that time, as a real-time tracker would. The\\n**time-late** model sees the whole recorded window, including bearings after the gate point, and\\nso is a post-hoc reconstruction.\\n\\nEach ratio is judged with a paired bootstrap 95% interval: pass if the whole interval is below 0.5,\\nfail if it is wholly at or above, otherwise inconclusive. Further checks guard the result: a model\\ntrained on shuffled labels must score like naive (leakage), each recurrent model must beat a\\nnon-recurrent control, and time-late must not be worse than instantaneous. How these combine is on\\nthe [evaluation](/docs/evaluation#g1--checks-and-how-they-combine) page.\\n\\n## What was trained\\n\\nFour small models over the same masked 240-row input window (see [models](/docs/algorithms/models)),\\nplus one leakage run, trained on GitHub-hosted runners (4 cores, no GPU). Every model had\\n`max_epochs` 60, patience 3 on validation loss and a 5.5 h wall-clock cap (`max_wall_s` 19,800;\\n2 h for the shuffled run). Epochs are counted from 0, so \\"60 (best 58)\\" means the 59th epoch gave\\nthe kept weights.\\n\\n| Model                         | Role                             | Layers                                | Epochs (best) | Stop reason    | Wall time  | s / epoch | Runner CPU                |\\n| ----------------------------- | -------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- | ------------- | -------------- | ---------- | --------- | ------------------------- |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`          | instantaneous variant (causal)   | LSTM 96                               | 60 (58)       | `max_epochs`   | 4 h 36 min | 276       | AMD EPYC 9V45             |\\n| `bilstm-time-late`            | time-late variant (whole window) | BiLSTM 96                             | 25 (23)       | `wall_clock`   | 5 h 23 min | 775       | AMD EPYC 9V74             |\\n| `cnn-causal-control`          | control for instantaneous        | 3 \xd7 causal Conv1D 64, k = 15          | 21 (17)       | early stopping | 53 min     | 152       | AMD EPYC 9V74             |\\n| `mlp-control`                 | control for time-late            | MLP 256-256 over the flattened window | 24 (20)       | early stopping | 7 min      | 17        | AMD EPYC 7763             |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous-shuffled` | leakage check                    | LSTM 96 on shuffled labels            | 8 (4)         | early stopping | 1 h 22 min | 617       | Intel Xeon Platinum 8370C |\\n\\nData: 100,000 scenarios (seed 101), \u03c3 = 0.5\xb0, no dropouts, initial range 2\u201320 km log-uniform;\\n80,000 train, 10,000 validation, 10,000 test, plus 10,000 held-out scenarios at 20\u201330 km.\\n\\nThe `bilstm-time-late` row is attempt 1. Under the protocol below it was continued once, from the\\nepoch-23 weights with no wall-clock cap, and stopped early after 50 epochs (best 46); the other\\nfour models are unchanged. One configuration per model; nothing was tuned. In attempt 1 the\\ntime-late configuration was started twice with identical settings (one job cancelled, one\\ncompleted).\\n\\n### Every run (FR-009)\\n\\n- [38131616871](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38131616871), `train/i1`, 11 Oct:\\n  all five models. Instantaneous, CNN, MLP and shuffled completed; these four are the models of\\n  record. The time-late job was cancelled after \u2248 5 h 15 min; the g1 job then stopped at once\\n  (`time-late: training has not finished`). Test split not scored.\\n- [38152208243](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38152208243), `train/i1`, 11 Oct:\\n  time-late only, attempt 1 (5 h 24 min job, AMD EPYC 9V74); the other four reused. Its g1 job\\n  scored the test split but could not push its results branch; informational only (same models\\n  as the attempt-1 re-score).\\n- [38153046011](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38153046011) and\\n  [38154023864](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38154023864), `train/ckpt-smoke`:\\n  checkpoint smoke tests (MLP); g1 stopped at model collection. Test split not scored.\\n- [38165442543](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38165442543), `train/i1`: cancelled\\n  25 s after it was queued; no job ran.\\n- [38178916962](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38178916962): re-score\\n  (`evaluate-g1.yml`) of the committed models, failed before any model was scored. The test\\n  features, regenerated at schema 0.5.0, carry a different header digest from the 0.4.0 training\\n  data, so prediction refused them (\\"normalisation differs\\"). Fixed by PR #27 (accepts a\\n  schema-version-only relabel).\\n- [38182021648](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38182021648): re-score with PR #27,\\n  **attempt 1 of record**, `experiments/20261011-g1-r2/`.\\n- [38208186337](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38208186337), `train/i1-continue`,\\n  12 Oct: the pre-registered time-late continuation, over two run attempts (jobs listed under\\n  attempt 2 below). Its final g1 job scored the test split into `experiments/20261012-g1/`, the\\n  training run\'s own folder (train/i1 code, no bootstrap; its `verdict.json` reads `fail`):\\n  **informational only**.\\n- [38275763335](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38275763335): re-score of all five\\n  committed models on main code (`evaluate-g1.yml`), **attempt 2 of record**,\\n  `experiments/20261012-g1-r2/`.\\n\\n## Attempt 1: results at the gate point (zig + 300 s, test split)\\n\\nExperiment `20261011-g1-r2`, re-scored from the committed weights by run 38182021648. n = 9,784\\nscenarios; 216 excluded because their window ends before the gate point; the same scenarios for\\nevery estimator.\\n\\n| Estimator                              | Range median | Range P90 | Course median | Course P90 | Speed median | Speed P90 | Gross-error rate |\\n| -------------------------------------- | ------------ | --------- | ------------- | ---------- | ------------ | --------- | ---------------- |\\n| naive                                  | 38.4%        | 161.5%    | 90.0\xb0         | 163.0\xb0     | 4.30 kt      | 7.67 kt   | 35.7%            |\\n| classical (per-timestep least squares) | 4.6%         | 29.2%     | 1.9\xb0          | 23.7\xb0      | 0.64 kt      | 5.19 kt   | 5.7%             |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`                   | 16.1%        | 39.6%     | 9.4\xb0          | 41.7\xb0      | 2.16 kt      | 5.59 kt   | 5.4%             |\\n| `bilstm-time-late`                     | 21.2%        | 49.6%     | 11.5\xb0         | 48.4\xb0      | 2.84 kt      | 6.28 kt   | 9.9%             |\\n| `cnn-causal-control`                   | 29.3%        | 69.7%     | 23.0\xb0         | 78.4\xb0      | 3.81 kt      | 7.33 kt   | 20.1%            |\\n| `mlp-control`                          | 26.3%        | 75.7%     | 16.8\xb0         | 65.6\xb0      | 3.39 kt      | 7.05 kt   | 18.7%            |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous-shuffled`          | 38.6%        | 122.6%    | 82.9\xb0         | 159.5\xb0     | 4.33 kt      | 7.66 kt   | 34.9%            |\\n\\nG1 threshold: 0.5 \xd7 naive median = 19.2%.\\n\\nClassical non-converged rate at the gate point: 2.0% (non-converged rows count as +\u221e). Classical\\nis far ahead of every model (a median 3.5\xd7 lower than the instantaneous model\'s); G1 asks only\\nwhether the models beat the prior, and closing that gap is G3\'s question.\\n\\n### Intervals\\n\\nPaired bootstrap of the ratio model / naive median range error at zig + 300 s (2,000 resamples of\\nthe 9,784 test scenarios, seed 20261011). A G1 check passes only if the whole 95% interval is below\\n0.5.\\n\\n| Variant       | Model median | Naive median | Classical median | Ratio to naive | 95% interval   | G1 check |\\n| ------------- | ------------ | ------------ | ---------------- | -------------- | -------------- | -------- |\\n| instantaneous | 16.1%        | 38.4%        | 4.6% (0.119)     | 0.419          | [0.407, 0.431] | pass     |\\n| time-late     | 21.2%        | 38.4%        | 4.6% (0.119)     | 0.551          | [0.538, 0.565] | fail     |\\n\\nThe classical ratio (in brackets) is a point value; no interval was computed for it. The interval\\nreflects which test scenarios were drawn, not how a re-trained model would score (one training\\nseed per model).\\n\\n### Checks\\n\\nMachine verdict: **fail**, because `g1-time-late` and `time-late-not-worse` failed.\\n\\n| Check                         | Result | Detail (median range error; naive and classical beside each)                                            |\\n| ----------------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\\n| `g1-instantaneous`            | pass   | 16.1% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.419 [0.407, 0.431] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                          |\\n| `g1-time-late`                | fail   | 21.2% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.551 [0.538, 0.565] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                          |\\n| `time-late-not-worse`         | fail   | time-late worse at 20 of 20 gated curve points (every 60 s to 20 min); 3 later points skipped (n < 500) |\\n| `instantaneous-beats-control` | pass   | zig+300s: 16.1% vs CNN 29.3%; final: 16.4% vs 29.3% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%)        |\\n| `time-late-beats-control`     | pass   | zig+300s: 21.2% vs MLP 26.3%; final: 24.3% vs 28.2% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%)        |\\n| `shuffled-labels-at-naive`    | pass   | 1.005 \xd7 naive (38.6% vs 38.4%; allowed 0.9\u20131.1)                                                         |\\n\\n**Error against time since the zig.** Naive is flat (38\u201349% median throughout). Classical falls\\nfast: 22.6% one minute after the zig, 4.6% at five minutes, 1.6% at fifteen. The instantaneous\\nmodel falls from 24.5% at one minute to 16.1% at five and levels off near 15% at ten to fifteen\\nminutes. The time-late model has the same shape but sits above it everywhere (26.0%, 21.2%,\\n19.0%), although it sees more data at every point. Rounds 1\u20132 read this as the signature of an\\nundertrained model, not of a pipeline that hides the future from it (see the panel\'s checks\\nbelow); round 3 withdrew that explanation for the continued model, which trained to early stopping\\nand is still worse (see the caveat). Interactive curves are on\\nthe [results page](/docs/results/iteration-1).\\n\\n### By range band and zig angle\\n\\nMedian range error at zig + 300 s, with each model\'s ratio to naive in brackets. Bands are by the\\nmean range over the window, so a few test scenarios fall outside 2\u201320 km. Slices with n < 200 are\\nnot interpreted.\\n\\n| Slice          | n     | naive  | classical     | instantaneous | time-late     |\\n| -------------- | ----- | ------ | ------------- | ------------- | ------------- |\\n| range 0\u20132 km   | 132   | 603.7% | 0.7% (0.001)  | 29.9% (0.050) | 53.5% (0.089) |\\n| range 2\u20135 km   | 1,640 | 150.8% | 2.0% (0.013)  | 17.2% (0.114) | 26.2% (0.174) |\\n| range 5\u201310 km  | 3,705 | 23.2%  | 3.8% (0.166)  | 13.6% (0.588) | 18.6% (0.802) |\\n| range 10\u201320 km | 3,741 | 32.5%  | 7.2% (0.222)  | 16.2% (0.498) | 20.0% (0.615) |\\n| range 20\u201330 km | 557   | 58.5%  | 10.4% (0.178) | 28.7% (0.490) | 33.0% (0.564) |\\n| range 30+ km   | 9     | 71.8%  | 8.2% (0.114)  | 27.0% (0.377) | 37.9% (0.529) |\\n| zig 30\u201350\xb0     | 3,232 | 38.7%  | 6.4% (0.166)  | 17.1% (0.441) | 21.6% (0.557) |\\n| zig 50\u201370\xb0     | 3,286 | 37.9%  | 4.3% (0.113)  | 16.0% (0.423) | 21.0% (0.554) |\\n| zig 70\u201390\xb0     | 3,266 | 38.7%  | 3.5% (0.091)  | 15.3% (0.395) | 21.1% (0.545) |\\n\\nThe per-band ratios mostly measure naive, not the models: naive guesses the middle of the prior,\\nso it is very wrong at short range and nearly right at 5\u201310 km. In absolute terms the models show\\nthe prior\'s U-shape (best at 5\u201310 km, worse at both ends), whereas classical\'s error grows steadily\\nwith range, as the geometry predicts (lower bearing rate at long range). Larger zigs help\\nclassical (6.4% \u2192 3.5%) and, weakly, the instantaneous model (17.1% \u2192 15.3%); the time-late model\\nis flat across zig angle (21.0\u201321.6%).\\n\\n### Held-out geometry (20\u201330 km initial range, reported, not gated)\\n\\n10,000 scenarios outside the training range; at zig + 300 s, n = 9,769 (231 excluded).\\n\\n| Estimator            | Range median | Range P90               | Gross-error rate | Ratio to naive |\\n| -------------------- | ------------ | ----------------------- | ---------------- | -------------- |\\n| naive                | 59.7%        | 70.2%                   | 77.6%            | 1              |\\n| classical            | 18.8%        | \u221e (12.6% non-converged) | 23.9%            | 0.315          |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous` | 42.4%        | 57.6%                   | 28.5%            | 0.710          |\\n| `bilstm-time-late`   | 45.9%        | 59.9%                   | 37.1%            | 0.768          |\\n\\nOutside the training range the models lose most of their advantage over naive (ratios 0.71 and\\n0.77, against 0.42 and 0.55 in range), while classical keeps a median of 19% at the cost of more\\nnon-converged solutions. The panel read this as the models being held towards the training prior\\nrather than extrapolating the geometry. G2 is to include a range-extrapolation check.\\n\\n## Attempt 1: why the time-late model was undertrained\\n\\nAll models shared the same budget in **time**, not in epochs: a 5.5 h wall-clock cap per model,\\ninside GitHub\'s 6 h job limit. The bidirectional LSTM runs the window in both\\ndirections and has twice the instantaneous LSTM\'s parameters (82,948 vs 41,476); one epoch took\\nabout 775 s against 276 s. The cap therefore stopped it after 25 epochs while the instantaneous\\nmodel ran all 60. Its validation loss was still falling (0.592, 0.565, 0.571 over the last three\\nepochs; best at epoch 23).\\n\\nThe panel compared like with like. On the post-zig validation rows (the rows that are scored), the\\ntime-late model at epoch 23 had a loss of 0.406; the instantaneous model at the same epoch had\\n0.478 and needed until epoch 31 to reach 0.405. At equal training, time-late was ahead.\\n\\n## What the panel found in attempt 1 (rounds 1\u20132)\\n\\nFive independent agent reviewers (TMA geometry, estimation and numerics, evaluation statistics,\\nreproducibility, red team) reviewed attempt 1 over two rounds; the full record, with every finding\\nand response, is in\\n[`experiments/20261011-g1-r2/review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261011-g1-r2/review.md).\\n\\nIn plain words:\\n\\n- **The pipeline is sound.** Padding does not leak into the bidirectional pass (padded and\\n  unpadded windows give identical outputs); predictions line up with their scenarios; the causal\\n  models ignore bearings after the time they estimate; the time-late model does use later bearings\\n  (cutting them off makes it worse, 21.2% \u2192 27.2%).\\n- **The instantaneous pass is real.** No truth reaches the inputs or the normalisation, naive is not\\n  artificially weak, every comparison uses the same 9,784 scenarios, and the shuffled-label model\\n  trained down to the \\"know nothing\\" level and scores like naive.\\n- **Reproducible.** A reviewer regenerated the data and re-scored the committed weights locally: the\\n  verdict file came out byte-identical, naive and classical metrics byte-identical, model metrics\\n  within 1.3 \xd7 10\u207b\u2075 relative.\\n- **The time-late fail is a budget effect** (one reviewer rejected in round 1 until this was\\n  recognised). Calling it \\"ML not extracting the signal\\" would overstate it. The first result is\\n  recorded as: _G1 not passed as run; the instantaneous variant met the criterion; the time-late\\n  variant failed under a 25-epoch wall-clock budget and is plausibly undertrained; no evidence that\\n  the time-late signal is absent._\\n- **But no rule allowed a re-run.** Turning \\"undertrained\\" into \\"try again\\" needed an explicit\\n  amendment, decided after seeing the result and written down before any new training. That is\\n  what the user decided (below), and round 2 approved the protocol with all five reviewers at\\n  approve-with-findings.\\n\\nSmaller findings, reported rather than fixed now: pre-zig rows, never scored, carry 64% of the\\ntime-late model\'s training loss; model manifests lack a git commit field (provenance\\nis recorded in the review instead); training budgets should be set in epochs, not wall-clock time,\\nbecause runner CPUs vary; the shuffled model\'s median course error (82.9\xb0) is a little below\\nchance (\u2248 90\xb0), a weak bearing-as-course prior rather than leakage.\\n\\n## The decision and the continuation protocol\\n\\nThe user decided on 12 October, after the first G1 result and before any further training, to\\ncontinue the time-late model **once**, to the same 60-epoch budget the instantaneous model had. SRD\\n\xa75 was amended to allow exactly this, and the protocol was fixed in\\n[`review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261011-g1-r2/review.md#user-decision-2026-10-12-after-round-1)\\nbefore the run started:\\n\\n1. **One change only:** no wall-clock cap. Training resumes from the committed epoch-23 weights\\n   (digest checked) and runs to epoch 60 or early stopping (patience 3). Epoch 24 of the first run\\n   is dropped.\\n2. **Unchanged:** data, splits, training seed, architecture, loss, learning rate, batch size. The\\n   other four models are reused untouched; the instantaneous model is not retrained.\\n3. **Optimizer:** Adam\'s state was never saved in attempt 1, so it restarts once, at epoch 24, and\\n   epochs 24\u201326 do not count towards patience. From then on Adam\'s state is saved with every\\n   checkpoint and restored if a job is interrupted, so a resumed run matches an uninterrupted one\\n   (tested: identical weights).\\n4. **Scoring:** the verdict of record comes from re-scoring all five committed models with\\n   `evaluate-g1.yml` on main; the test split is not touched until training completes.\\n5. **Outcomes, fixed in advance:** both intervals wholly below 0.5 and shuffled labels at naive \u2192\\n   **G1 pass**, even if `time-late-not-worse` still fails (the record then says \\"time-late not shown\\n   better than instantaneous\\"). A ratio fail of the continued model is **final**: no further\\n   continuation, seed or architecture change, and the project stops per SRD \xa75.\\n6. **Record:** attempt 1 (0.551) stays recorded as a valid attempt. Every job of the continuation is\\n   listed with its run id, epochs covered and runner CPU.\\n\\n## Attempt 2: the continued time-late model\\n\\nExperiment `20261012-g1-r2`, re-scored by run\\n[38275763335](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/actions/runs/38275763335) on main code (commit\\nbb4525b). The four reused models are byte-identical to attempt 1; their metrics differ from\\nattempt 1\'s re-score by at most 8.4 \xd7 10\u207b\u2076 relative (float32 inference on AVX2, then AVX-512), and\\nnaive and classical are identical.\\n\\n**Training.** Run 38208186337, branch `train/i1-continue`, commit 7c1c579. Before training, the\\nseed step checked the epoch-23 weights (digest 2c5f8784\u2026) and a configuration that differed from\\nattempt 1 only in `max_wall_s` (19,800 \u2192 86,400).\\n\\n| Attempt | Job          | Epochs | Runner CPU                | Outcome                                           |\\n| ------- | ------------ | ------ | ------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\\n| 1       | 114676289268 | 24\u201341  | AMD EPYC 7763             | 345-min step timeout; last checkpoint at epoch 41 |\\n| 1       | 114785366608 | \u2013      | \u2013                         | g1 stopped before scoring; test split untouched   |\\n| 2       | 114789157629 | 42\u201349  | Intel Xeon Platinum 8573C | early stopping, best epoch 46                     |\\n| 2       | 114832540726 | \u2013      | \u2013                         | g1 succeeded; informational folder `20261012-g1/` |\\n\\nThe first job\'s log reads \\"resuming bilstm-time-late after epoch 23 (optimizer state restarts;\\npatience not counted for epochs 24\u201326)\\", the second\'s \\"resuming bilstm-time-late after epoch 41\\n(optimizer state restored)\\". Epochs took about 1,100 s on the AMD runner and 870 s on the Intel one. Epochs 0\u201323 ran in attempt\\n1 (run 38152208243, AMD EPYC 9V74), so training crossed three CPU families and a retrain will not\\nreproduce the weights byte for byte. The committed weights (c52b628c\u2026) are the artefact of record;\\nre-scoring them is deterministic, and a reviewer reproduced this experiment folder byte for byte.\\nExtracted log lines are in\\n[`experiments/20261012-g1/training-logs/`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/tree/main/experiments/20261012-g1/training-logs).\\n\\nTraining stopped after 50 epochs (best 46), stop reason `early_stopping`, against the\\ninstantaneous model\'s 60. Validation loss at the best epoch was 0.441 (attempt 1\'s best: 0.565 at\\nepoch 23).\\n\\n**The epoch-48 spike.** Train and validation loss jumped at epoch 48 (validation 0.447 \u2192 0.535,\\nthen 0.448 at epoch 49). It is a training instability, not a resume artefact: the instantaneous\\nmodel, never resumed, had one at epoch 40. The scored weights are from epoch 46, before it. The\\nspike contributed to the patience stop at epoch 49, so the \\"equal epoch budget\\" was in effect 50\\nagainst 60; whether epoch 48 would otherwise have improved is unknown. Gradient clipping is an\\niteration-2 candidate.\\n\\n### At the gate point (zig + 300 s, test split)\\n\\nn = 9,784 scenarios (216 excluded), the same for every estimator, as in attempt 1.\\n\\n| Estimator                      | Range median | Range P90 | Course median | Course P90 | Speed median | Speed P90 | Gross-error rate |\\n| ------------------------------ | ------------ | --------- | ------------- | ---------- | ------------ | --------- | ---------------- |\\n| naive                          | 38.4%        | 161.5%    | 90.0\xb0         | 163.0\xb0     | 4.30 kt      | 7.67 kt   | 35.7%            |\\n| classical                      | 4.6%         | 29.2%     | 1.9\xb0          | 23.7\xb0      | 0.64 kt      | 5.19 kt   | 5.7%             |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`           | 16.1%        | 39.6%     | 9.4\xb0          | 41.7\xb0      | 2.16 kt      | 5.59 kt   | 5.4%             |\\n| `bilstm-time-late` (continued) | 17.3%        | 43.5%     | 9.6\xb0          | 41.1\xb0      | 2.39 kt      | 5.71 kt   | 7.3%             |\\n| `cnn-causal-control`           | 29.3%        | 69.7%     | 23.0\xb0         | 78.4\xb0      | 3.81 kt      | 7.33 kt   | 20.1%            |\\n| `mlp-control`                  | 26.3%        | 75.7%     | 16.8\xb0         | 65.6\xb0      | 3.39 kt      | 7.05 kt   | 18.7%            |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous-shuffled`  | 38.6%        | 122.6%    | 82.9\xb0         | 159.5\xb0     | 4.33 kt      | 7.66 kt   | 34.9%            |\\n\\nG1 threshold: 0.5 \xd7 naive median = 19.2%. Classical non-converged rate at the gate point: 2.0%.\\nClassical is still far ahead: its median is 3.5\xd7 lower than the instantaneous model\'s and 3.8\xd7\\nlower than the time-late model\'s.\\n\\n### Intervals, both attempts\\n\\nPaired bootstrap of model / naive median range error at zig + 300 s (2,000 resamples of the 9,784\\ntest scenarios, seed 20261011). A G1 check passes only if the whole 95% interval is below 0.5.\\nMedians are of range error; classical\'s ratio to naive is 0.119, a point value (no interval\\ncomputed).\\n\\n| Attempt, variant | Model | Naive | Classical | Ratio | 95% interval   | G1 check |\\n| ---------------- | ----- | ----- | --------- | ----- | -------------- | -------- |\\n| 1, instantaneous | 16.1% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.419 | [0.407, 0.431] | pass     |\\n| 1, time-late     | 21.2% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.551 | [0.538, 0.565] | fail     |\\n| 2, instantaneous | 16.1% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.419 | [0.407, 0.431] | pass     |\\n| 2, time-late     | 17.3% | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 0.450 | [0.438, 0.464] | pass     |\\n\\nThe time-late upper bound is 0.036 below 0.5, about 5 bootstrap standard errors; its point\\nestimate is about 7 below. The statistics reviewer\'s independent bootstrap agreed (time-late\\n[0.4377, 0.4649], instantaneous [0.4074, 0.4313]). The intervals reflect which test scenarios were\\ndrawn, not how a re-trained model would score: each variant was trained once.\\n\\n### Checks\\n\\nMachine verdict: **inconclusive**, from `time-late-not-worse` (tiered rule 2). `verdict.json` is\\nleft exactly as the code wrote it; the pass is the panel\'s application of rule 1.\\n\\n| Check                         | Result | Detail (median range error; naive and classical beside each)                                     |\\n| ----------------------------- | ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\\n| `g1-instantaneous`            | pass   | 16.1% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.419 [0.407, 0.431] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                   |\\n| `g1-time-late`                | pass   | 17.3% vs limit 19.2%; ratio 0.450 [0.438, 0.464] (naive 38.4%, classical 4.6%)                   |\\n| `time-late-not-worse`         | fail   | more than 5% worse than instantaneous at 14 of 20 gated points; 3 later points skipped (n < 500) |\\n| `instantaneous-beats-control` | pass   | zig+300s: 16.1% vs CNN 29.3%; final: 16.4% vs 29.3% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%) |\\n| `time-late-beats-control`     | pass   | zig+300s: 17.3% vs MLP 26.3%; final: 18.5% vs 28.2% (naive 38.4% / 40.9%, classical 4.6% / 2.4%) |\\n| `shuffled-labels-at-naive`    | pass   | 1.005 \xd7 naive (38.6% vs 38.4%; allowed 0.9\u20131.1)                                                  |\\n\\n**Error against time since the zig** (median range error, test split):\\n\\n| Time since zig | n      | naive | classical | instantaneous | time-late (continued) | time-late / instantaneous |\\n| -------------- | ------ | ----- | --------- | ------------- | --------------------- | ------------------------- |\\n| 60 s           | 10,000 | 40.3% | 22.6%     | 24.5%         | 24.1%                 | 0.984                     |\\n| 300 s          | 9,784  | 38.4% | 4.6%      | 16.1%         | 17.3%                 | 1.075                     |\\n| 600 s          | 6,512  | 39.1% | 2.3%      | 14.9%         | 15.7%                 | 1.054                     |\\n| 900 s          | 3,116  | 43.4% | 1.6%      | 14.5%         | 15.5%                 | 1.069                     |\\n| 1200 s         | 516    | 48.9% | 1.4%      | 17.0%         | 16.5%                 | 0.973                     |\\n\\nThe continuation closed most of the gap to the instantaneous model (attempt 1\'s time-late model:\\n26.0%, 21.2% and 19.0% at one, five and ten minutes) but did not cross it.\\n\\n### By range band and zig angle\\n\\nMedian range error at zig + 300 s, ratio to naive in brackets. Slices with n < 200 are not\\ninterpreted.\\n\\n| Slice          | n     | naive  | classical     | instantaneous | time-late (continued) |\\n| -------------- | ----- | ------ | ------------- | ------------- | --------------------- |\\n| range 0\u20132 km   | 132   | 603.7% | 0.7% (0.001)  | 29.9% (0.050) | 46.2% (0.077)         |\\n| range 2\u20135 km   | 1,640 | 150.8% | 2.0% (0.013)  | 17.2% (0.114) | 21.2% (0.141)         |\\n| range 5\u201310 km  | 3,705 | 23.2%  | 3.8% (0.166)  | 13.6% (0.588) | 15.8% (0.683)         |\\n| range 10\u201320 km | 3,741 | 32.5%  | 7.2% (0.222)  | 16.2% (0.498) | 15.6% (0.480)         |\\n| range 20\u201330 km | 557   | 58.5%  | 10.4% (0.178) | 28.7% (0.490) | 27.5% (0.471)         |\\n| range 30+ km   | 9     | 71.8%  | 8.2% (0.114)  | 27.0% (0.377) | 30.3% (0.422)         |\\n| zig 30\u201350\xb0     | 3,232 | 38.7%  | 6.4% (0.166)  | 17.1% (0.441) | 18.3% (0.473)         |\\n| zig 50\u201370\xb0     | 3,286 | 37.9%  | 4.3% (0.113)  | 16.0% (0.423) | 17.0% (0.449)         |\\n| zig 70\u201390\xb0     | 3,266 | 38.7%  | 3.5% (0.091)  | 15.3% (0.395) | 16.6% (0.429)         |\\n\\nThe time-late model\'s deficit is at 2\u201310 km; at 10\u201330 km it is level with, or slightly ahead of,\\nthe instantaneous model. Both models still follow the training prior\'s U-shape across range bands,\\nwhereas classical\'s error grows steadily with range.\\n\\n### Held-out geometry (20\u201330 km initial range, reported, not gated)\\n\\nn = 9,769 at zig + 300 s (231 excluded).\\n\\n| Estimator                      | Range median | Range P90               | Gross-error rate | Ratio to naive |\\n| ------------------------------ | ------------ | ----------------------- | ---------------- | -------------- |\\n| naive                          | 59.7%        | 70.2%                   | 77.6%            | 1              |\\n| classical                      | 18.8%        | \u221e (12.6% non-converged) | 23.9%            | 0.315          |\\n| `lstm-instantaneous`           | 42.4%        | 57.6%                   | 28.5%            | 0.710          |\\n| `bilstm-time-late` (continued) | 43.9%        | 58.5%                   | 31.1%            | 0.735          |\\n\\nAs in attempt 1, both models are held towards the training prior outside the training range.\\n\\n## What the panel decided (round 3)\\n\\nThe same five specialist reviewers judged the continuation. The full record is in\\n[`experiments/20261012-g1-r2/review.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/experiments/20261012-g1-r2/review.md).\\n\\n| Reviewer                | Attempt 1 (rounds 1\u20132)                   | Round 3               | Round 3 re-invitation |\\n| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------- | --------------------- | --------------------- |\\n| TMA geometry            | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve-with-findings |\\n| Estimation and numerics | reject \u2192 approve-with-findings (round 2) | approve-with-findings | approve               |\\n| Evaluation statistics   | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve-with-findings |\\n| Reproducibility         | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve               |\\n| Red team                | approve-with-findings                    | approve-with-findings | approve-with-findings |\\n\\nAll five said the evidence supports **G1 pass under pre-registered rule 1**. The reproducibility\\nreviewer re-scored the committed weights locally and got `verdict.json`, `bootstrap.json`,\\nmetrics, curves and held-out results byte-identical; the red team found no protocol deviation,\\nleakage or selection between attempts (one continuation, one re-score).\\n\\n**One major finding (M1, red team; geometry agreed).** The SRD \xa75 G1 row still said a time-late\\nconsistency failure makes G1 inconclusive, and did not carry rule 1; as the SRD wins conflicts,\\nthe pass needed it there. The row was amended to carry rule 1 as the user decided it on\\n12 October before the run. Rule 1 was committed at 05:29:44Z and published in PR #28 at\\n05:30:16Z, 17 s after the run was triggered (05:29:59Z) but before any continuation epoch finished\\n(about 05:49Z) and about 8 h before the test split was scored (13:25Z). The round-1 explanation\\nof the consistency failure (undertraining) is withdrawn for the continued model.\\n\\nMinor findings, answered in the record: the caveat now states the gap rather than only \\"not shown\\nbetter\\"; the epoch-48 spike (above); a cross-process resume test for the bidirectional layer,\\nwhich a reviewer ran (byte-identical weights) and which iteration 2 adds to the suite; the per-job\\nrecord and both CPUs, now in the review; re-scored manifests lack `cpu_model`, which\\n`evaluate-g1.yml` will record in iteration 2. The re-invitation corrected the wording of the gap,\\nthe margin and the spike. No reviewer rejects and no blocker or major finding is open; the\\nverdict\'s `decided_by` names round 3.\\n\\n## Outcome: G1 passes, with a caveat\\n\\n**G1 passes** under pre-registered rule 1 (SRD \xa75 as amended 2026-10-12). Both ratio intervals\\nlie wholly below 0.5, instantaneous 0.419 [0.407, 0.431] and time-late (continued to 50 epochs)\\n0.450 [0.438, 0.464], and the shuffled-label model sits at naive. A small network does extract\\nrange information from bearings alone, in real time and in reconstruction, though at the gate\\npoint it is still about 3.5\xd7 (instantaneous) and 3.8\xd7 (time-late) worse than the classical solver.\\n\\n**Caveat, recorded with the pass:**\\n\\n- **Time-late not shown better than instantaneous.** It is measurably worse: at zig+300s,\\n  0.173 vs 0.161, a ratio of 1.075 [1.046, 1.108]. Its median is higher at 18 of the 20 gated\\n  points, by more than 5% (up to 9%) at 14 of them (120\u2013480, 600 and 720\u20131020 s). It is lower\\n  only at zig+60s (0.984 [0.964, 1.006]) and zig+1200s (0.973 [0.864, 1.126], n 516); neither\\n  difference is significant.\\n- **Not explained by undertraining.** The continued model trained to early stopping.\\n- **The BiLSTM under-uses the future.** Its error profile is shaped like a causal estimator\'s.\\n  Masking and the backward pass are verified correct, and truncating the future does change\\n  outputs, but weakly.\\n- **One training draw per variant.** Training-seed variance is unquantified.\\n\\n## What G1 cannot tell us\\n\\n- The targets move at constant velocity and the ownship zig is instantaneous; nothing here says how\\n  the models cope with manoeuvres or gradual turns.\\n- \u03c3 = 0.5\xb0 and no dropouts only; noise and gaps are iteration 2\'s question (G2).\\n- The time-late model\'s estimate at zig + 300 s uses bearings recorded after that time. It is a\\n  post-hoc reconstruction, not a real-time solution.\\n- The interval covers test sampling, not training variation: each model was trained once, and\\n  weights differ across runner CPU families.\\n- The time-late model was trained in three stretches on three CPU families with one optimizer\\n  restart; the instantaneous model in one. The protocol equalised the epoch budget, not the\\n  training path, and early stopping ended the time-late model at 50 epochs against 60.\\n- The shuffled-label test rules out leakage through labels and the training harness; truth hidden\\n  in the inputs is ruled out by a separate counterfactual test.\\n\\nThe full list, with mitigations, is in\\n[`specs/002-iteration-1-signal/g1-limits.md`](https://github.com/debrief/Gitma/blob/main/specs/002-iteration-1-signal/g1-limits.md).\\n\\n## Next: iteration 2\\n\\nIteration 2 (spec 003) runs the same models on \u03c3 = 1\u20132\xb0 with 10% dropouts, judged at G2: median\\nrange error below 20% and course below 10\xb0 five minutes after the zig, speed below 1 kt ten\\nminutes after, at \u03c3 = 1\xb0. At \u03c3 = 0.5\xb0 the instantaneous model is at 16.1% and 9.4\xb0 (zig + 300 s)\\nand 1.83 kt (zig + 600 s), against classical\'s 4.6%, 1.9\xb0 and 0.31 kt, so speed is furthest off.\\nLeads from this gate:\\n\\n- Pre-zig rows carry 64% of the time-late model\'s training loss but are never scored.\\n- Gradient clipping, against spikes like the one at epoch 48.\\n- Both models still follow the training prior by range band, and are held to the training prior\\n  on the held-out 20\u201330 km set; G2 is to include a range-extrapolation check.\\n- Why the bidirectional model under-uses the future, before time-late is relied on.\\n- Engineering: training budgets in epochs rather than wall-clock time; the runner CPU recorded in\\n  re-scored manifests; a cross-process bidirectional resume test; a git commit in model manifests."},{"id":"/2026/10/11/g0-pipeline-verified","metadata":{"permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/11/g0-pipeline-verified","source":"@site/blog/2026-10-11-g0-pipeline-verified.md","title":"Iteration 0: G0 passes \u2014 the pipeline is verified","description":"The first gate asks a narrow question: can we trust the data before training anything on it?","date":"2026-10-11T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[],"readingTime":1.12,"hasTruncateMarker":false,"authors":[],"frontMatter":{"title":"Iteration 0: G0 passes \u2014 the pipeline is verified","date":"2026-10-11T00:00:00.000Z"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"Iteration 1: G1 passed (panel round 3) with a caveat","permalink":"/Gitma/pr/86/blog/2026/10/12/iteration-1"}},"content":"The first gate asks a narrow question: can we trust the data before training anything on it?\\n\\n**Verdict: G0 pass** (machine verdict; team review pending). Experiment:\\n`experiments/20261011-g0/`.\\n\\n- 100,000 noise-free scenarios generated (seed 42); the 10,000-scenario test split was scored.\\n- The least-squares solver recovered truth on **10,000 / 10,000** scenarios. Worst cases: 2.9\xd710\u207b\u2074\\n  relative range, 0.016\xb0 course, 0.002 kt speed (tolerances 10\u207b\xb3, 0.1\xb0, 0.01 kt). Median range\\n  error 1.9\xd710\u207b\u2077.\\n- The naive baseline reproduced exactly across two independent fits.\\n\\n## Yardsticks for iteration 1\\n\\nOn the same noise-free test set, the naive baseline\'s median range error is 40% (P90 139%), its\\nmedian course error 90\xb0 and its gross-error rate 37%. Any model must beat these to pass G1.\\n\\nOn noisy data (\u03c3 0.5\u20132\xb0, up to 10% dropouts) the classical solver\'s median range error is about 5%,\\nwith P90 \u2248 43% and gross-error rate \u2248 9%. None of those gross errors were optimiser failures:\\nstarting from the true state never found a lower cost. The tail comes from the information in the\\nbearings, not from a weak solver.\\n\\n## Caveat\\n\\nPassing G0 shows that generator and solver agree. On its own it would not catch an error shared by\\nboth; hand-computed geometry tests cover that separately.\\n\\nGeneration plus the G0 run took about 70 seconds on a laptop-class machine."}]}}')}}]);